regress\artifacts\armbfiwidth\panel_20260908\grok.txt:1:I'll verify the claimed difference first — if UBFX/SBFX cannot produce a negative width and BFI can, that decides whether `#510`'s form transfers. Then I'll check the site, the decoder, and any UNPREDICTABLE precedent in the ARM handlers.The handlers and decoder are in `cpu_arm_instr.c`; next I’ll read those sites plus the disassembler encoding so the width-domain claim is checked against the tree, not the brief.Section 2’s encoding split looks real in the decoder; I’ll now count the 1024 pairs, grep UNPREDICTABLE handling, and check whether width-32 BFI is actually guest-visible like `#510`.The encoding split is confirmed; next I’ll brute-force the 468/465 counts, read the UNPREDICTABLE handlers, and check whether width-32 BFI is already a guest-visible miss like `#510`.**Verdict: Section 2 is true — `#510`’s form does not transfer. Extend it with a nonpositive-width guard. This is a definedness repair for the negative-width half (the ARM value is unsettleable from this tree) and, for one valid pair, the same width-32 wrong-answer fix `#510` already shipped.**
