regress\artifacts\armbfiwidth\panel_20260908\grok.txt:76:Destination **unchanged**. After the repair it is a full-register copy. Different symptom from UBFX (which wrote 0); same root; same class of correction `#510` already shipped for `UBFX/SBFX #0,#32`.
regress\artifacts\armbfiwidth\panel_20260908\grok.txt:80:I did not re-plant a BFI word this session. The width-32 lowering is transferred from `#510`’s measurement of `(1 << width) - 1` in the same translation unit, not re-measured on `X(bfi)`.
regress\artifacts\armbfiwidth\panel_20260908\grok.txt:82:`1UL` vs `1U`: C99 allows `unsigned int` to be 16 bits. Every host this project builds on has 32-bit `int`, and `#510` shipped `1U`. Matching that is the right consistency for this round; widening the literal here while leaving the siblings on `1U` is a different change.
regress\artifacts\armbfiwidth\panel_20260908\grok.txt:106:`#510` cannot reach negative width. That part of the brief is right. It is not the whole story, because those two handlers **do** face a different UNPREDICTABLE, and they do not NOP it.
regress\artifacts\armbfiwidth\panel_20260908\grok.txt:110:**1. The sibling bitfield handlers, fifty lines up (no `lsb + width` check anywhere in this file).** `#510`’s own comment: a width-32 extract returns the whole register only for a legal field (`lsb = 0`); “the decoder does not enforce that.” Architecturally, `lsb + width > 32` is the UBFX/SBFX analogue of BFI’s `msb < lsb`. The model executes the shift-and-mask and writes Rd. **UNPREDICTABLE here does not mean “leave dest unchanged.”**
regress\artifacts\armbfiwidth\panel_20260908\grok.txt:126:`#510`’s finding transfers: **`1 << 31` and `1U << 31` give the same mask after `- 1`.** A guest-value row at width 31 stays green if someone reverts `1U` → `1`. Only UBSAN or a source pin kills that.
