regress\artifacts\armbfxwidth32\pass2_01a0339\codex.txt:1106:regress/pass2_ledger.tsv:139:c0c7f55	2026-09-07	PASS 2 on the #510 diff, run BEFORE this SHA existed: panel_20260907_192452 (preserved: regress/artifacts/armbfxwidth32/pass2_panel/), brief with the whole 3-hunk product diff and the 11-row list inlined.  SEATS: codex, grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (6); the kimi CLI WALLED on a 403 weekly limit so the seat is COVERED, not lost; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED, held for the 2026-09-09 adjudication by standing owner directive.  grok's verdict: RATIFY THE REPAIR; DO NOT RATIFY THE COMMENTS, THE "11/11 PINS BOTH HALVES" READING, OR THE 496-COUNT.  All four packet-fed seats independently confirmed the mask is correct at widths 1, 31 and 32 and that the `&&' reorder changes no guest value.  *** THIS RECEIPT IS ITSELF LATE AND THE LATENESS COST A PERMANENT FALSE RECORD, which is the single most important thing in it.  codex's pass-2 BODY was read AFTER the commit had launched, so its finding landed too late to stop the message: `c0c7f55' asserts that the pc control holds "on EVERY row, w32 included".  THAT IS FALSE.  Only `pc adv', `mid pc' and `w32 pc' assert pc, and all three are MOV/UBFX -- NO SBFX ROW CHECKED PC AT ALL, and the SBFX rows are precisely the ones whose expected failure value is ALSO exactly zero, i.e. the case the pc control exists to disambiguate.  A commit message cannot be edited; the CHANGELOG correction and the probe comment shipped in the follow-up ARE the record.  The lesson is not "read faster": it is that a pass-2 seat's answer is not discharged by its VERDICT LINE, which this project already knows -- a verdict line is written against the brief's own framing and is the LEAST likely part of an answer to carry a correction. ***  THREE ONE-LINE ESCAPES, and the valuable part is why two of them cannot be closed by any row.  (1) `1U' -> `1' in the mask ternary: `w31' stays green because it is the very wrap this round's own comment describes, and `w32' never evaluates the shift -- `1 << 31' and `1U << 31' give the SAME mask after the `- 1'.  (2) restoring `topBitMask' above the `if': `s w32' stays green because with a correct 32-bit mask `x |= ~mask' is `x |= 0' -- so `s w32' pins the MASK, not `topBitMask' and not the hoist, and the probe comment calling it "the second-UB row" was wrong about what that row can see.  (3) `if (width < 32)' with no bit test: `s mid' stays green because every byte field of 0x89abcdef has bit 7 set.  THE THIRD WAS CLOSED IN-ROUND by `s clear' (SBFX #1,#4 -> field 0x7, bit 3 clear).  THE FIRST TWO ARE DISCLOSED, and the disclosure is structural rather than a row-set gap: both are DEFINEDNESS repairs and definedness has no guest-visible value on a non-trapping host.  *** THEY ARE NOT UNKILLABLE IN PRINCIPLE, and I wrote "unchecked" about this one grep from the answer: build-asan carries -fsanitize=address,undefined, so a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL pins both -- fixed source emits no shift diagnostic, the `1U'->`1' mutant does.  That is a second binary and a STDERR oracle, i.e. a NEW MEASUREMENT LANE, so it is filed as `ubsanshiftlane' for its own round rather than bolted onto one whose pass 2 has already run.  A lane added after a pass is a lane nobody reviewed. ***  ONE CLAIMED BREACH REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT: glm asserted "no row ever takes the sign-extension branch, so deleting `x |= ~mask' still scores 11/11".  Built with a pre-registered prediction and measured 10/11, KILLED BY `s mid' ALONE -- `s mid's field is 0xde, whose bit 7 IS set.  Restore verified by cmp against SEC, sentinel cleared.  *** THE 496-COUNT WAS WRONG AND TWO SEATS CAUGHT IT INDEPENDENTLY.  The round claimed "496 of 1024 encodings give a NEGATIVE shift count" for `bfi'.  Enumerated: 465 strictly negative, 31 exactly zero, 496 = the `msb < lsb' population.  At width 0 the shift `1 << 0' is DEFINED and the mask becomes 0 -- semantically wrong, not undefined.  glm derived it from the brief's own width formula; grok reached it separately; codex confirmed.  The conclusion is unchanged and the number had already propagated into four records, all corrected. ***  AND MY OWN PATCH INVALIDATED MY OWN CITATIONS: grok noted the brief's decoder cites were the PRE-comment line numbers; after the diff they moved, and after the pass-2 edits they moved again -- THREE VALUES IN ONE EVENING for one unchanged expression, which is why no line number for it appears in the CHANGELOG block.  FOLLOW-UP SHIPPED SEPARATELY (probe half applied, source half is comment-only): `s w32 pc' and `s w31' added, because SBFX at width 31 was UNTESTED and the round's headline claim -- "only the width-32 case is observable" -- rested on a UBFX width-31 row and generalised to SBFX with no evidence.  bfi at cpu_arm_instr.c X(bfi) DELIBERATELY UNTOUCHED and disclosed: it carries the same shift-UB shape and is out of this round's scope by the one-site rule.
