regress\artifacts\armbfxwidth32\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:232:Premise: "grep -n '#510' src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c returns nothing" — the point is the round hasn't shipped. Not checkable. Not central.
regress\artifacts\armbfxwidth32\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:255:Hmm, hold on — but wait. Let me reconsider: is `1 << 31` really UB in C99? Yes — the classic. Many compilers even warn ("left shift of 1 by 31 places cannot be represented in type 'int'" — clang gives this warning for constant shifts). For a variable shift, UBSan flags it as "shift exponent 31 is too large for 32-bit 'int'"? Actually UBSan's shift check: it flags both exponent ≥ width AND (for signed left) overflow. Hmm, UBSan's `-fsanitize=shift` catches `1 << 31`? Let me think: UBSan shift-base check... `1 << 31` — I believe UBSan reports "left shift of 1 by 31 places cannot be represented in type 'int'" — yes, that's the runtime version of clang's constant warning. Yes, shift sanitize includes the signed-overflow-in-shift case.
regress\artifacts\armbfxwidth32\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:321:**"The most recent correction tag is #312" / "grep #510 returns nothing"** — asserts round hasn't shipped; not checkable from packet; irrelevant to truth of the claim. Skip (mention only if needed).
regress\artifacts\armbfxwidth32\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:374:Wait — actually for a UBSan witness, width=31 trips `-fsanitize=shift`? Let me think: UBSan's shift check: for `E1 << E2` with signed E1, it checks exponent < width AND (for signed left shift) checks overflow? Clang's `-fsanitize=shift` includes shift-exponent and shift-base; `1 << 31` → shift-base: "left shift of 1 by 31 places cannot be represented in type 'int'" — yes, this is caught by shift-base sanitizer (part of -fsanitize=shift). So UBSan flags width=31 too. Good — worth telling the author: the sanitizer witness doesn't even need width=32; `UBFX rd, rn, #1, #31` (legal, common) trips it.
regress\artifacts\armbfxwidth32\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:428:- At width=31 (also UB), the practical result is the correct mask on two's-complement hosts → no wrong answer, but UBSan/`-fsanitize=shift` flags it. So the sanitizer witness needs no exotic encoding: `#1,#31` trips it; the wrong-answer witness needs `#0,#32`.
