regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4007:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:8061:     "note": "TWO SIDE QUESTIONS SETTLED, and both corrections went in the direction that keeps the substance.\n\n(1) THE ASan CLAIM: refuted on its premise, upheld on its conclusion, and a THIRD reason found that neither I nor the reporting seat had. build_asan.sh:38-39 does set -fsanitize=address,undefined, and the artifact carries eleven __ubsan_handle_* imports INCLUDING __ubsan_handle_add_overflow -- the exact handler an int++ overflow calls. So 'no -fsanitize flag' is false; the seat had grepped the CONSUMER (gate_asan_sweep.sh) rather than the PRODUCER. *** But gate 9 still cannot catch this, and the reason I gave (timeout 25 vs a 42.95 s floor) was only half of it: DEVINIT(footbridge) NEVER CREATES A struct timer AT ALL. ***  Only a guest write to TIMER_x_LOAD or TIMER_x_CONTROL|ENABLE does, and a construction sweep runs no guest -- so timer_tick0 is never called and the counter never increments, timeout or no timeout. Either reason alone is fatal.\n\n(2) THE '~7.9 s FOR FOUR TIMERS' FIGURE: confirmed impossible, and the seat found WHERE THE NUMBER CAME FROM, which is better than refuting it. 2^31 / (4 x 1048576 x 65) = 7.876 s -- the single-element floor divided by four. The counters are per-timer (:73), each callback increments its own element (:86-92), and the catch-up budget is per-timer too (timer.c:281, inside the per-timer loop), so four timers overflow four DIFFERENT elements at their own rates. True floors: 42.950 s cats (2^31/50,000,000, machine_cats.c:56-57) now measured three times at 43.03/43.10/44.58, and 33.686 s netwinder (2^31/63,750,000, machine_netwinder.c:56).\n\nONE REFINEMENT worth recording: a hard floor of 31.51 s exists from the catch-up ceiling (2^31/68,157,440), but it needs a timer at or above 68.16 MHz, which neither machine's emulated_hz permits. cats's is guarded by `if (== 0)` so a config-file emulated_hz word could reach it; netwinder's is unconditional. Neither is guest-reachable, so severity is unchanged.",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:5173:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:21875:   "state_note": "*** OVERTAKEN BY `#475`, AND MEASURED WITH A CONTROL RATHER THAN READ. ***\n\nThe row said, MEASURED: with `atapi_len = 3` and a four-byte read, `atapi_len` lands on -1\nand `atapi_received` on 4 where `data_in_len` is 3 -- so `== 0` never fires again and the\ndevice claims it delivered more bytes than the transfer holds.\n\nMEASURED AT HEAD, through the REAL REGISTER PATH (`dev_wdc_access`, a four-byte data-port\nread), not by calling internals:\n\n    shipped        atapi_len = 0   received = 3   data_in_len = 3     BOTH halves correct\n    even control   atapi_len = 0   received = 4   data_in_len = 4     unaffected\n\n*** AND THE DISCRIMINATING CONTROL, because an `ok` is otherwise equally explained by a probe\nthat cannot see the defect: REMOVE `#475`'s clamp and the queue's exact numbers come back. ***\n\n    clamp removed  atapi_len = -1  received = 4   data_in_len = 3     the row's own figures\n\nCAUSE: `#475` added `if (consumed > d->atapi_len) consumed = d->atapi_len;` IMMEDIATELY\nbefore the subtraction.  That clamp does exactly what this row's open question was asking\nfor -- it saturates the counter AND keeps `atapi_received` truthful -- so the two halves the\nrow treated as a design choice were both already closed by one line.\n\n*** THE OWNER WAS ASKED WHICH FIX TO TAKE AND CHOSE 'FIX BOTH COUNTERS'.  THAT IS PRECISELY\nWHAT #475's CLAMP ALREADY DOES.  The decision was sound and the defect was gone. ***  The\nrow's MEASURED note predated that round and nothing re-ran it -- the same shelf-life problem\nthis project keeps finding in audits.\n\nNO ROW ADDED, AND THE ADDITION LICENCE IS WHY.  The clamp-removed control reds NINE rows, and\ntwo of them pin exactly what a new row would:\n\n    odd chunk: the count stops at zero        atapi_len = -1, want 0    the saturation half\n    odd chunk: received never exceeds len     received = 4,  want 3     the TRUTHFULNESS half\n\nplus `width: received stops at data_in_len`.  So `#475` shipped carriers for both halves.  A\nnew row would kill nothing a shipped row does not, which is 'it cannot hurt' -- the one\nwarrant this fork does not accept.  The probe was worth building as a MEASUREMENT and is not\nworth shipping as a ROW.\n\nNO PRODUCT CHANGE.  The round is records only, and that is the finding.\n\n*** THE grok SEAT CAUGHT AN L4 IN THE FIX COMMENT BEFORE IT SHIPPED, AND CORRECTED THE\nADJUDICATOR ON A SEPARATE POINT. 2026-08-27. ***\n\n**L4 (fixed pre-ship): the comment conflated two different numbers.**  It said an INQUIRY of\n37 *'drove atapi_len to -1'*.  It does not: **the 3-byte fixture lands on -1; the worked\nINQUIRY-37 path lands on -3** (nine 4-byte reads leave 1, the tenth takes 4).  Both are real\nand they belong to different scenarios.  The comment now names both and attributes each.\n\n*Caught because the seat re-derived the worked path instead of reading the sentence.  It also\nnoticed the working tree was dirty with the uncommitted clamp and reviewed against HEAD\nanyway, which is the right instinct and not one the brief asked for.*\n\n**IT ALSO CORRECTED THE FABLE ADJUDICATION ON OPTION E.**  Fable rejected E (count successful\npops) partly on the ground that `wdc_get_inbuf`'s failure signal is IN-BAND and so\nindistinguishable.  grok: *the `(uint64_t)-1` is distinguishable from a data byte IN THE\nHANDLER (0..255); the masking objection applies to the GUEST-VISIBLE byte, not to a pop\ncounter.*  **E is implementable.**  It is still rejected, on the two grounds that survive --\nbyte-identical to A on a clean ring, and advancing a TRANSFER cursor on FOREIGN bytes on a\ndirty one -- plus a cost grok measured by reading: **four pop sites across two endian\nbranches**, against one line for A.\n\n*Same disposition, one of its three warrants withdrawn.  Recorded because a wrong reason gets\nreused, and this one was inside an adjudication.*\n\n**Three cautions from the same seat, all adopted as constraints on this round:**\n* do NOT add a floor for an already-negative `atapi_len` -- from a clean command A maintains\n  `>= 0`, so extra saturates are unlicensed by the addition rule;\n* do NOT clamp `received` to `data_in_len` as a companion to B -- mid-chunk that completes\n  EARLY and drops the rest of the transfer;\n* do NOT even-pad at the SCSI layer and call this closed.\n\n*** FIXED AND MEASURED 2026-08-27. Kill table 6 KILLED + 1 MEASURED EQUIVALENT; detector\n30 rows, 0 failures. Commit HELD pending the full roster. ***\n\n```\n  PRE-FIX   FAIL odd chunk: the count stops at zero      atapi_len = -1, want 0\n            FAIL odd chunk: received never exceeds len   received = 4, want 3\n  POST-FIX  ok   odd chunk: the count stops at zero      0\n            ok   odd chunk: received never exceeds len   3\n```\n\n**THE FIX (option A), and it is the tree's own idiom rather than an invention:**\n`if (consumed > d->atapi_len) consumed = d->atapi_len;` before both updates. VERIFIED by\nopening the file: `dev_asc.c:610-614` -- `#283`'s clamp half -- is the same shape, clamping\nthe step to the remainder before the move.\n\n| mutant | verdict | killed by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Mb_revert` (b) remove the clamp | KILLED 2/4 | both end-state rows |\n| `Mc_null` (c) null medium | KILLED | compile |\n| `Md_assign` min -> assignment | **KILLED 2/4** | **ONLY the mid-walk rows** |\n| `Md_plusone` predicate + 1 | KILLED 2/4 | both end-state rows |\n| `Md_saturate` option B instead | KILLED 1/4 | the truthfulness row |\n| `Md_lenonly` clamp len, not received | KILLED 1/4 | the truthfulness row |\n| `Md_ge` `>` -> `>=` | *ESCAPED* | MEASURED EQUIVALENT -- see below |\n\n*** THE ROW THE PANEL TOLD ME I OWED IS THE ONLY ROW THAT CATCHES `Md_assign`. ***  The\nFable seat named that escape -- the min degraded to a plain assignment -- and predicted the\nend-state rows would sweep it clean because the transfer does still end at 0 of 37.\n**Measured: both end-state rows stayed GREEN and only the intermediate-state rows went\nred.**  Without that row the round would have shipped a detector blind to a one-token edit.\n\n**`Md_ge` IS A GENUINE EQUIVALENCE, predicted by the same seat and confirmed by\ninspection rather than assumed.**  The only input where `>=` and `>` differ is\n`consumed == atapi_len`, and there the guarded statement assigns `consumed = consumed` --\na provable no-op.  So this is not mis-built, not a dead target and not a mis-cut row; it is\nthe fourth disposition the carrier already recognises from `#449`, and it is recorded as a\nRESULT rather than as coverage debt.\n\n**Both `Md_saturate` and `Md_lenonly` die on the TRUTHFULNESS row alone**, which is the\nmeasured justification for keeping the two halves as separate rows: a combined row could\nnot distinguish option A from option B.\n\n*** FABLE ADJUDICATION 2026-08-27: OPTION A, ONE ROUND -- and it found a FIFTH option,\nrefuted my framing of Q3, and named the escape that would sweep my planned detector. ***\n\n**THE REAL WARRANT FOR A, replacing the underflow argument that died:** `atapi_received` has\nTWO LIVE MID-TRANSFER CONSUMERS -- the re-arm length and the refill's source offset\n`data_in[atapi_received + j]`.  A guest makes `atapi_len` odd mid-chunk with a single 1-byte\nread, then overshoots with a 4-byte read while `received < data_in_len` is STILL TRUE.\nUnder B/C the re-arm then resumes at a skewed offset, **permanently skipping up to 3 real\ntransfer bytes that the guest was served as ring-empty 0xff**, while the counters claim full\ndelivery.  *That is `#283`'s exact defect shape re-created.*\n\n**A FIFTH OPTION EXISTS -- E: count SUCCESSFUL POPS**, transplanting `#338`'s\n`transfer_bufpos` idiom (`dev_mb89352.c:260-266`, verified).  Rejected on three read\ngrounds: on a polluted ring it advances a TRANSFER-indexed counter on FOREIGN bytes,\ncorrupting the same two consumers that make B wrong; on a clean ring it is byte-identical to\nA; and it is not one line, because `wdc_get_inbuf`'s failure signal is IN-BAND\n(`(uint64_t)-1`, indistinguishable from data after masking).\n\n*** AND THE DECISIVE FIND: OPTION A IS THE TREE'S OWN IDIOM, NOT AN INVENTION. ***\nVERIFIED by opening the file -- `dev_asc.c:610-614` is `#283`'s clamp half:\n\n```c\n    if (d->xferp->data_out_offset + len2 > d->xferp->data_out_len) {\n            len2 = d->xferp->data_out_len - d->xferp->data_out_offset;\n    }\n```\n\nThat is 'clamp the step to what remains, before the move' -- option A in different variable\nnames.  A TRANSFERRED WARRANT, and the addition-licence rule is satisfied by it.\n\n**Q3 REFUSED AS FRAMED, correctly.**  `atapi_received` is not 'bytes the guest received' --\nits three consumers all use it as *how far the walk of `data_in[]` has got*.  The refill\npushes exactly `atapi_len` bytes of `data_in`; every other byte in the ring during DATAIN is\nforeign by construction.  **So A is correct under BOTH ring states, and never needed the\nring-empty premise I built the question around.**\n\n*** THE ESCAPE THAT WOULD SWEEP MY PLANNED DETECTOR: `consumed = d->atapi_len;` *** -- the\nmin degraded to a plain assignment, a plausible future 'simplification'.  Both my end-state\nrows pass (0 and 37) while the FIRST 4-byte read consumes the whole transfer and completes\nafter 4 bytes delivered.  **The detector owes an INTERMEDIATE-STATE row** -- after nine\n4-byte reads: not completed, byte-count registers read 1.  Second escape: `consumed >\natapi_len + 1`, killed by a 2-byte read of a 1-byte remainder.  `>` -> `>=` is an\nEQUIVALENCE (self-clamp is identity) and is recorded as one per the non-dying-mutant rule.\n\n*** L4 -- TWO CITATION ERRORS OF MINE, CAUGHT BY THE FABLE SEAT AND VERIFIED AGAINST THE\nSOURCE BEFORE CORRECTING. 2026-08-27. ***\n\n**(a) `:746` is NOT REQUEST SENSE.**  It is READ(6)'s block count (`retlen = cmd[4]`, with\n`if (retlen == 0) retlen = 256;` below it).  REQUEST SENSE is at `:954`, and it carries a\nMIN-18 clamp -- odd is still guest-choosable there (19), so the conclusion survives and the\ncitation did not.\n\n**(b) MODE SENSE IS NOT A HEAP OVER-READ, and I said the class was.**  It allocates\n`retlen + 100` and then does `xferp->data_in_len -= 100;  /*  Restore size.  */` -- so\n`data_in_len` is 100 bytes SMALLER than the allocation and an over-read past it lands in\nREAL SLACK.  **The 'exact allocation, so an over-read is a genuine heap over-read' sentence\nis true of INQUIRY and REQUEST SENSE and FALSE of MODE SENSE.**\n\n*This is the citation-form rule biting exactly as written: I cited a cross-file line NUMBER\nfor a durable claim instead of a verified-unique grep anchor, and the number was wrong.\nThe severity claim is unchanged -- INQUIRY with allocation length 37 still reaches it -- but\nthe BLAST RADIUS is smaller than I wrote, and a wrong blast radius is what a reader would\nhave carried forward.*\n\n*** THE PANEL REPLACED MY DEAD ARGUMENT WITH A BETTER ONE. Cloud seats complete\n2026-08-27, 4 of 4 answered -- the kimi substitute came back after two 503s. ***\n\n**Tally on my wrong premise ('B/C open the underflow'): glm and kimi REFUTED it, deepseek\nand minimax RATIFIED it. 2-2, and the code sides with the refuters** -- the subtraction is\ninside `received < data_in_len`, which an over-count makes false. Settled by reading, which\nis the standing rule, not by the tally.\n\n**THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT SURVIVES, from kimi: the MID-CHUNK overshoot.**  On a non-final\nchunk `atapi_len` is 32768, so uniform-width reads never overshoot -- but WIDTH-MIXING\ndoes: consume 32767 in byte reads, then issue a 4-byte read.  Under B/C the counters\novershoot and the re-arm STILL FIRES, because mid-transfer `received < data_in_len` is\ntrue.  The refill then indexes `data_in[received + j]` with a skewed `received` -- **a\nguest-visible hole in the delivered stream, one per overshoot point.**  Option A cannot\nproduce it because the counters never leave the transfer.\n\n*Note what makes this reachable: `#473`'s multi-chunk road.  Before that round no transfer\nwalked past the first re-arm, so a mid-chunk overshoot had nowhere to go.*\n\n**OPTION D IS REJECTED, and on a point neither I nor glm raised.**  A 4-byte port read must\nreturn four bytes, so D either SYNTHESISES padding -- byte-identical to the empty ring's\n0xff, but it **suppresses the latched over-read warning, which is the only witness-signal\nthat a guest did this** -- or it 'refuses', which the architecture cannot express on a port\nread.  *D-done-right converges to A-minus-the-warning, with a second site touched.*\n\n**AN ASSUMPTION TO PIN BEFORE COMMITTING, also from kimi:** option A is sound only while\n`consumed` equals the ACTUAL pops.  Today `len == 3` gives `consumed == 2`.  If the memory\nsubsystem ever delivers a genuine 3-byte read that pops three, the same class reopens in\nthe OTHER direction as an UNDER-count.  Either pin that in-tree or confirm `len == 3` never\nreaches this handler.\n\n*** TWO OF THREE CLOUD SEATS RATIFIED A WRONG PREMISE I PUT IN THE BRIEF -- AND THE BRIEF\nCARRIED THE ANTI-RATIFICATION INSTRUCTION. Measured 2026-08-27. ***\n\n| seat | on 'B/C open the underflow' |\n|---|---|\n| glm | **REFUTED it** -- the guard fails, so the re-arm is skipped |\n| deepseek | ratified: *'B and C make the gate fire while leaving the overcount, which opens the underflow'* |\n| minimax | ratified: *'the user's reasoning ... is correct, and is what makes B/C a NEW hazard'* |\n\n**The code settles it and the majority is wrong.** With option B on the odd case\n(`atapi_len` saturated to 0, `received` 4, `data_in_len` 3): the outer `atapi_len == 0`\nfires, then the INNER `received < data_in_len` evaluates `4 < 3` = FALSE, so control takes\nthe else branch and the subtraction -- which lives in the true branch -- never runs.\n\n**WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A SCORE:** the brief stated the underflow as its strongest\nargument for option A. Both ratifying seats verified the ARITHMETIC (the sign-and-width\nwalk to 0xFFFFFFFF is correct) and neither checked the CONTROL FLOW that makes it\nunreachable. minimax's own table calls it *'REASONED - verified by sign-and-width walk'* --\na true statement about the expression, offered as a verification of the claim.\n\n*The carrier already records that a brief that presents a DERIVATION directs seats to check\nthe arithmetic instead of the premise. This is that, with the premise-audit instruction\npresent and losing 2-1 anyway. The instruction is not sufficient when the brief supplies a\nderivation attractive enough to check.*\n\n**glm was the only seat that asked what GUARDS the expression rather than what the\nexpression computes.**\n\n*** TWO SEATS DISAGREED ON THE SAME QUESTION AND IT WAS SETTLED BY READING, NOT BY VOTE.\n2026-08-27. ***\n\n| seat | claim |\n|---|---|\n| glm | B/C **bypass** the underflow -- the guard fails, so the re-arm is skipped |\n| deepseek | B/C **open** it -- 'the gate fires while the overcount remains' |\n\n**glm is right.** The nesting settles it:\n\n```c\n    if (d->atapi_len == 0) {                        /*  B/C make THIS fire  */\n            if (d->atapi_received < data_in_len) {  /*  ...but THIS still guards  */\n                    atapi_len = data_in_len - atapi_received;   /* the subtraction */\n```\n\nThe unsigned subtraction is INSIDE `received < data_in_len`, and an over-count is exactly\nthe condition that makes that test FALSE. So the branch is skipped and the underflow never\nexecutes. deepseek is right that B/C make the OUTER gate fire and wrong that this reaches\nthe subtraction.\n\n**AND MY OWN BRIEF WAS WRONG IN EXACTLY DEEPSEEK'S WAY.** I derived the underflow from the\ntypes (`int` = `size_t` - `size_t`) and presented it as the strongest argument for option\nA. It is not an argument for anything: **the underflow is unreachable under every option**,\nso it is not a discriminator and must not be used as one.\n\n*A hazard found by reading TYPES is a hypothesis about control flow, and the control flow\nhas to be checked separately. The guard that makes it unreachable is the line immediately\nabove it, and I had read that line -- in a different context, for a different question.*\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES AS A REAL DISCRIMINATOR** is glm's tail/counter argument: on a dirty ring a\nfour-byte read pops FOUR real bytes and advances `inbuf_tail` by four, while option A\ncredits three -- desynchronising the ring from the bookkeeping. Option D (clamp the read\nLENGTH before the pop) is the only option that keeps them in lockstep.\n\n*** A CLAIM I PUT IN THE PASS-1 BRIEF IS REFUTED, BY THE glm SEAT, AND I RE-DERIVED THE\nREFUTATION BEFORE ACCEPTING IT. Corrected 2026-08-27. ***\n\nI wrote that options B and C -- which let the command complete while leaving\n`atapi_received` over-counted -- would OPEN the re-arm's unsigned-underflow path\n(`atapi_len = data_in_len - atapi_received` assigned to an int). **That is false.**\n\nOn the LAST chunk the arm sets `atapi_len = data_in_len - atapi_received`, so\n`atapi_len + atapi_received == data_in_len` and the two move in lockstep thereafter\n(`-= consumed` / `+= consumed`). Therefore **`atapi_len < 0` if and only if\n`atapi_received > data_in_len`** -- and in exactly that case the re-arm's own guard\n`if (atapi_received < data_in_len)` is FALSE, so the block is skipped and the underflow\nsubtraction never executes. On a NON-last chunk `atapi_len` is clamped to 32768 and cannot\nreach zero from below at all.\n\n**B and C BYPASS the hazard rather than open it.** The underflow remains unreachable under\nevery option, so it is not a discriminator between them and must not be used as one.\n\n*** AND THE SEAT SUPPLIED A BETTER ARGUMENT THAN THE ONE IT REMOVED, which is the part\nworth keeping. ***  Against option A (clamp `consumed` to the remainder) it observes: if\nthe ring is DIRTY -- and this week proved it can be, via the unguarded data-port write arm\n-- then a four-byte read of a three-byte remainder pops FOUR REAL bytes and `inbuf_tail`\nadvances by four, while option A credits only three. **That desynchronises the ring from\nthe bookkeeping**, losing a byte the guest actually received. Option D (clamp the read\nLENGTH before the pop) keeps tail and counters in lockstep and is the only option that\ndoes, if the dirty-ring premise holds.\n\n*My brief asked whether a dirty ring breaks option A. It does, but not by the mechanism I\nproposed -- not an under-count of undelivered bytes, but a tail/counter divergence.*\n\n*** SEVERITY RAISED 2026-08-27 BY READING THE SCSI LAYER: THE ODD LENGTH IS GUEST-CHOSEN,\nNOT AN EDGE CASE. ***\n\n`diskimage_scsicmd.c` sets the transfer length from a SINGLE GUEST BYTE:\n\n```\n  :370   INQUIRY        retlen = xferp->cmd[4];        <- allocation length, 0..255\n  :371   ...clamped to a MINIMUM of 36, never to an even value\n  :572   MODE SENSE     retlen = cmd[4], or cmd[7]*256 + cmd[8]\n  :589                  retlen += 100;                 <- still freely odd\n  :746   REQUEST SENSE  retlen = cmd[4];\n```\n\nand `scsi_transfer_allocbuf(&xferp->data_in_len, &xferp->data_in, retlen, 1)` makes that\nvalue `data_in_len` directly.\n\n**WORKED PATH, no re-arm and no oversized transfer involved:** a guest issues INQUIRY with\nallocation length **37**.  `atapi_len` starts at 37; nine four-byte reads consume 36 and\nleave 1; the tenth four-byte read makes `consumed` 4 and drives `atapi_len` to **-3**.\n`== 0` never fires again, the command never completes, and `atapi_received` reaches 40\nagainst a `data_in_len` of 37.\n\n*This was filed as a residual of `#473` and is considerably more than that.  `#473` added a\nsecond road via an odd FINAL chunk; the FIRST road is a one-byte field in one of the most\nordinary SCSI commands there is.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-08-27, on the committed tree, in a scratch fixture. ***\n\n```\n  atapi_len = 3 (ODD), guest does a FOUR-byte read of wd_data\n  -> atapi_len      = -1        (wanted 0)\n  -> atapi_received =  4        (data_in_len is 3)\n```\n\n**TWO defects in one subtraction.**  The counter steps PAST zero, so `if (atapi_len == 0)`\nnever fires again and the command never completes -- and `atapi_received` ends up LARGER\nthan `data_in_len`, so the device claims it delivered more bytes than the transfer holds.\n\n**THIS IS THE `#341` CLASS SURVIVING AT THE SAME SITE.**  `#341` fixed `consumed` so it\ncounts what the reads actually POP (`len == 4 ? 4 : (len >= 2 ? 2 : 1)`) rather than what\nthe guest asked for.  It did NOT make the subtraction saturate, and its own comment\ndescribes exactly this symptom -- *\"let atapi_len step PAST zero, which its `== 0` test\nthen never catches\"* -- as the thing being fixed.  The mismatch it removed was\nlen-vs-consumed; the mismatch that remains is consumed-vs-REMAINING.\n\n**PRE-EXISTING, NOT INTRODUCED BY `#473`.**  A small odd transfer (MODE SENSE, REQUEST\nSENSE) reaches it with no re-arm at all.  But `#473` gives it a SECOND road: before that\nround every transfer over 32768 bytes died at the first re-arm, so a multi-chunk transfer\nwith an ODD FINAL CHUNK could never be walked to its end.  Now it can.  *Strictly fewer\ntransfers break than before -- this is a residual of a real improvement, not a regression,\nand it is filed rather than reverted.*\n\n**FOUND BY ATTACKING A PREMISE, WHICH IS WHY THE BRIEF ASKED FOR IT.**  `#473`'s pass-2\nbrief named the refill's \"the ring is empty at the re-arm\" claim as its load-bearing\npremise and asked seats to break it.  A grok working note -- *\"the re-arm's emptiness claim\nis only a drain-path invariant\"* -- prompted the trace.  **The emptiness claim itself HELD**:\n`head`/`tail` are never reset anywhere, but the packet path requires `inbuf_len == 12`\nEXACTLY, so a dirty ring can never issue a packet command and the arm is only reachable\nfrom an empty ring.  The counter defect was found on the way past.\n\n**THE FIX IS ONE LINE AND IS NOT YET MADE:** saturate the subtraction, or test `<= 0`.\nWhich one is a real question -- saturating hides the over-count in `atapi_received`, while\n`<= 0` completes the command but still reports having delivered 4 of 3 bytes.  **The\ntruthfulness half needs its own answer**, and this fork's own precedents (`#283` ASC,\n`#338` mb89352, `#341` here) all say the count reported has to be the count that happened.\n",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:5344:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23147-   "state_note": "*** AND ITS FIRST RUN PAID FOR IT THE SAME HOUR. ***\n\n#489's bare-deletion mutant under the shipped 19-row detector: **7 x heap-buffer-overflow, every one a WRITE**, five `0 bytes after 407-byte region` and two `0 bytes after 1-byte region`.  The stack runs from `main` through `sh_run_instr` and a guest `mov.w` store into `handle_command`, onto a buffer from `alloc_data`'s `malloc(guest cnt)`.  `regress/artifacts/gdromexitsix/asan_m0_20260828/`.\n\n*The FIRST attempt reported `6/19, zero reports` -- noise, not a negative.  LeakSanitizer fires at exit on every session and ASan's default exit code is 1, so every survival row read as a death while the genuine reports went to a pty the probe does not retain.  The tell was `ok surv` reading exit1 on the LEGAL length, which the mutant cannot affect.*\n\n*** FIXED 2026-08-28.  THE TREE BUILDS, RUNS, AND HAS ASAN LINKED. ***\n\nFour build attempts, each peeling one layer, every layer INVISIBLE to the propagation gate:\n  1. stale HEADERS   -- diskimage.c vs a diskimage.h missing nr_of_tracks, DISKIMAGE_MAX_SPT,\n                        struct diskimage_track.  Fixed by back-filling 474 .c/.h from SEC.\n  2. stale GENERATORS -- new sources against an OLD makeautodev.sh emitted\n                        `int devinit_Makefile.skel(...)`, a glob expansion the script's own\n                        comment warns about.  Fixed by syncing 16 scripts/.index/Makefile.skel.\n  3. stale GENERATED MAKEFILE -- OBJS predating dev_ne2000.c (a SEC-only file), giving\n                        `undefined reference to devinit_ne2000`.  Fixed by re-running\n                        ./configure with CFLAGS='-fsanitize=address,undefined\n                        -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g'; COPTIM verified to still carry the\n                        sanitizer afterwards, and the 15 prior Makefiles were backed up first.\n  4. none -- it built.\n\n*** MAKE_RC REPORTED 0 ON EVERY FAILED BUILD. ***  Caught only because the check was\n`[ -x gxemul ]`.  The carrier already says the exit status lies here; this is three more\ninstances in one session.\n\n**THE CAUSE IS STRUCTURAL AND OUTLIVES THIS TREE: AN INCREMENTAL PROPAGATION CHECK CANNOT\nSEE A STALE BASELINE.**  precommit_check.sh enforces propagation for `$CHANGED` -- the files\nTHIS commit touches -- which is deliberate and right (*a gate that hard-fails on a tree\nsomeone chose not to build is a gate that gets switched off*).  But the tree was ALREADY\nstale when the 2026-08-27 ruling landed, and nothing back-fills.  diskimage.h and\nmakeautodev.sh are touched by no recent round, so no gate ever required them.\n\n**The tree passed every commit's check while compiling nothing at all** -- worse than the\ngate comment's own fear of *a detector that has silently stopped detecting, worse than no\ndetector because it reassures*.\n\n*** OWNER DECISION 2026-08-28: a SOFT-WARNING full `diff -rq` of the .c/.h set, SKIPPED\nENTIRELY when the tree is absent. ***  Warning rather than hard-fail, and absent-is-skipped,\nso the absence-is-not-a-failure rule that keeps the gate switched on is preserved.  Filed as\n`asanbaselinedrift` to ship as its own harness round with its two negative controls.\n\nNOW UNBLOCKED: #489's headline claim.  Its bare-deletion mutant scored 12/19 with the host\nSURVIVING a 408-byte write past a malloc(0) -- which shows the write does not FAULT, not\nthat it is ABSENT.  An ASAN run settles that, and the tree to run it on now exists.\n\n*A check of mine scored the finished binary as having no ASan: `nm | grep -c __asan` = 0.\nWrong test -- the sanitizer symbols are in the SHARED LIBRARY.  `ldd | grep libasan` is the\ntest, and the build I nearly discarded was already correct.*\n\n*** DIAGNOSED AND FIXED 2026-08-28, AND THE CAUSE IS A GAP IN THE HARNESS RATHER THAN A\nSTALE FILE. ***\n\nMEASURED FIRST: `make -j8` in build-asan/ reported **MAKE_RC=0 AND PRODUCED NO BINARY** --\nthe documented trap, caught only because the check was `[ -x gxemul ]` rather than the exit\nstatus.  The real errors were in diskimage.c: `struct diskimage has no member named\nnr_of_tracks`, `DISKIMAGE_MAX_SPT undeclared`, `invalid use of undefined type struct\ndiskimage_track` -- a current .c against a STALE .h.\n\n*** THE CAUSE: THE PROPAGATION RULE IS INCREMENTAL AND THE TREE NEEDED A BASELINE. ***\nThe owner ruled on 2026-08-27 that build-asan/ joins the propagation set, and\nprecommit_check.sh implements that for `$CHANGED` -- the files THIS commit touches.  That\nis deliberate and well-reasoned (its own comment: *a gate that hard-fails on a tree someone\nchose not to build is a gate that gets switched off*).  **But the tree was already stale\nwhen the ruling landed, and nothing back-fills.**  `diskimage.h` is touched by no recent\nround, so no gate ever required it -- and it is what broke the build.\n\n**So the tree could pass every commit's check and still not compile.**  That is worse than\nthe gate comment's own fear -- it says a stale ASAN tree is *a detector that has silently\nstopped detecting, worse than no detector because it reassures*.  This one did not build at\nall, so it detected nothing, while every commit touching it reported green.\n\nMEASURED DIVERGENCE at diagnosis: **111 differing .c/.h files, 1 present only in SEC**\n(360 paths total, the rest .o and generated Makefiles).  No divergence is permitted for\nshared files -- unlike est/, build-asan has no allowlist -- so all 111 were staleness.\n\nFIXED by back-filling every .c/.h from GXEMUL-SEC/src (474 files copied, source divergence\nmeasured 0 afterwards), under a `.MUTANT` sentinel so an interrupted sync could not be\nmistaken for a clean tree.  Rebuilt with `rm src/cpus/*.o` first.\n\n*** WHAT REMAINS OPEN IS THE HARNESS GAP, NOT THIS TREE: an incremental propagation check\ncannot detect a stale BASELINE, and there is no periodic full comparison.  The next\nbaseline drift will be just as invisible. ***  A full `diff -rq` of the .c/.h set is cheap\nand would have caught this in one command; whether it belongs in the gate (and at what\ncadence, given the absence-is-not-a-failure rule) is an owner decision, not a round's.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS: `#489`'s strongest single result is the bare-deletion\nmutant scoring 12/19 with the host SURVIVING a 408-byte write past a `malloc(0)`.  The\nmeasuring seat stated its own limit -- that shows the write does not FAULT, not that it is\nABSENT.  An ASAN run is exactly what closes that gap.\n\n*** `build-asan/` DOES NOT BUILD AND HAS NO BINARY. Found 2026-08-27 while propagating\n#484, and it PREDATES that round -- `dev_wdc` appears ZERO times in the failure log. ***\n\n    make        103 errors, and it reported EXIT 0 -- the documented false-zero, again\n    build-asan/gxemul   DOES NOT EXIST\n    stale objects       222 .o files with no binary to link them into\n\nROOT CAUSE, verified rather than guessed: `build-asan/src/disk/diskimage.c` is IN SYNC\nwith SEC while `build-asan/src/include/diskimage.h` is NOT.  The new .c uses members the\nold .h does not declare -- `nr_of_tracks`, `total_disc_sectors`, `first_data_track_lba`,\n`DISKIMAGE_MAX_SPT`, `struct diskimage_track` -- each present in SEC's header and absent\nfrom this one.  **A .c was propagated without its header**, which is the no-VPATH rule\nobeyed halfway.\n\nAND IT IS NOT ONE FILE: **363 files under `build-asan/src` differ from SEC.**  This tree\nis broadly abandoned, not narrowly stale.\n\n*** THE CONSEQUENCE IS SILENT COVERAGE LOSS, THE SAME CLASS AS A GATE THAT SKIPS. ***\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` exists to run the machine-construction sweep under ASAN.  With no\nbinary it cannot do that -- and this project has already been bitten once by a gate\nskipping on a missing binary and scoring the skip as a pass.\n\nNOT FIXED IN #484, and deliberately: the stopping rule fixes a finding in-round only if it\nis a MEASURED FALSE PASS of this round or a WRONG RECORD.  This is neither -- it is a\npre-existing tree defect in a tree #484 does not measure against.  Folding a 363-file\nresync into a ring-buffer round is exactly how rounds stop converging.\n\nREMEDY when it is worked: re-derive the tree from SEC rather than patching the header.\nA tree 363 files out of date has no property worth preserving, and a header-only fix\nwould leave the other 362 to be discovered one build at a time.",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:6243:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28082:   "note": "*** A SURVIVAL ORACLE THAT A WRONG `exit(0)` SATISFIES IS NOT A SURVIVAL ORACLE. ***  Found by the codex seat while answering `sgideprimop`'s pass-1 brief, in shipped material it was not asked about.  `regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py` pins host survival with `row(..., how, \"exit0\", ...)` at **NINE sites across FIVE probe files**, derived rather than transcribed with `grep -rn 'how, \\\"exit0\\\"' regress/*.py`:  `sgi_de_width_probe.py` 4 (`lo_alive`, `lo_alive2`, `hi_alive`, `hi_rd_alive`), `gdrom_decline_probe.py` 2, `gdrom_default_probe.py` 1, `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` 1, `sgi_re_width_probe.py` 1.  *codex named three, in the one file it had been given; the class is three times that and spans devices it was never shown -- which is why the row is filed as a CLASS and the deriver is written down instead of the number.*  The oracle reads the process's exit status after the harness sends `quit`.  **An emulator that calls `exit(0)` on the guest access under test ALSO exits 0**, so the row cannot distinguish *the host survived and was then asked to quit* from *the host died quietly at exactly the moment the row exists to observe*.  **THE REPAIR codex NAMES:** assert the `alive` value captured BEFORE the harness sends `quit`, ideally followed by a post-trigger command whose result proves the emulator was still answering.  The probe already captures `alive` -- the rows simply do not use it.  *** RANKED AS COVERAGE DEBT, NOT AN L2, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE DOCUMENTED ONE. ***  These rows are in a SHIPPED detector that sits above the vacuity floor: it fails on the pre-fix build, on a verbatim reinstatement (14/25, eleven reds) and on a null medium.  The stopping rule files findings against shipped material and fixes only MEASURED FALSE PASSES and WRONG RECORDS.  **No mutant has been built that exploits this**, so it is a hypothetical edit in an uncovered cell -- filed, ranked below live defects in the same file.  *What would promote it to L2: a measured mutant that calls `exit(0)` on the declined path and sweeps the detector clean.  That is one build and is the natural first job for whoever takes this row.*  **CHECK THE CLASS BEFORE FIXING THE INSTANCE** -- `grep -rn 'exit0' regress/*.py` across the tree, since the idiom will have been copied; this project has twice corrected an instance and left its siblings standing.  *** THE COUNT IN THIS ROW WAS WRONG WHEN FILED -- 9, AND THE POPULATION IS 13.  Corrected 2026-08-31 by the adjudicating seat, which could not reproduce my figure by any method. ***  **THE DERIVER, and it is the fix as well as the count:** `grep -rn '\"exit0\"' regress/*.py`, counting `row(...)` want-sites -- **13 across 5 files**: `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` 5, `sgi_de_width_probe.py` 4, `gdrom_decline_probe.py` 2, `gdrom_default_probe.py` 1, `sgi_re_width_probe.py` 1 (that one INSIDE A LOOP, so its runtime rows exceed its textual site).  **My grep was `'how, \"exit0\"'` -- anchored on the VARIABLE NAME rather than the ORACLE VALUE -- and `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` uses `how1`, `how2`, `how3`, `how4`.** Four sites invisible to a pattern that looked precise.  *A row filed to adjudicate oracle blindness shipped with a count produced by a blind pattern; that is the frozen-number rule arriving inside the filing that names it.*  *** THE RANKING SURVIVES, AND THE SEAT GAVE IT A MEASURED REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THE BLINDNESS IS PER-ROW, NOT PER-DETECTOR. ***  `lo_sent` reads `printed(t, \"v0\") if alive else \"died\"`, so it reds on ANY death whatever the exit code -- verified by the seat, which built a decline-then-`exit(0)` mutant of `#498` and found it KILLED by the read-side sentinel rows even though all four `*_alive` rows passed it.  **So no shipped detector is floor-breached as a whole (not L2), and no record is made false where a session-mate positive row exists (not L3).**  Coverage debt, filed, med -- confirmed, on evidence rather than on the stopping rule alone.  **THE FIXING ROUND'S FIRST JOB IS THEREFORE A NARROWER CENSUS: which of the 13 lack a death-proof SESSION-MATE?**  Those are the live ones; the rest are redundant belt-and-braces and can be repaired cheaply or left.  *That is a smaller and better-aimed job than \"repair 13 rows\".*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: grep -h 'row(.*\"exit0\"' regress/*.py | wc -l = 24 on 2026-08-31\n\n*** THE COUNT WENT STALE WITHIN HOURS OF BEING FILED, BY THE FILER'S OWN EDIT. ***  Filed 2026-08-31 as THIRTEEN; derived the SAME DAY as TWENTY-FOUR, of which sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py alone holds ELEVEN because #499 pass 4 added seven.  That is this project's documented pattern exactly -- the rounds that consult a number are the rounds that change it -- so the title now carries the DERIVER and no literal.  (Demoted: 13, filed 2026-08-31, never a current claim.)  Per-file on that date: sgi_re_unmapped 11, gdrom_toclen 5, sgi_de_width 4, gdrom_decline 2, gdrom_default 1, sgi_re_width 1.\n\nAND THE CLASS BEARS ON #499's OWN PASS-4 ROWS.  Of the twelve rows pass 4 added, five are SURVIVAL-ONLY with an `exit0` want.  The READ side is covered -- `clip rd sent` reads `died` when the host is gone.  The WRITE side has NO post-death positive observable: surv wants exit0 and is satisfied, decl already counted its message before the exit.  A WRITE-SIDE-ONLY exit(0) is therefore PREDICTED to escape all 48 rows.  That is clause (d) shaped -- guard kept, its repair (falling through to the common tail) removed -- so it is NOT filed on my reading; it is QUEUED AS MUTANT E0W and will be measured.  Floor clause (b) is unaffected and measured green: a verbatim exit(1) reinstatement scores 18/48.\n\nAND A SECOND SITE FOUND THE SAME DAY BY THE lcabadval SEAT, which makes this a CLASS rather than a probe-local habit: on `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` (dev_lca.c) are bare fatal()+exit(1), and bus_isa wires the whole ISA PIC chain to them.  So ANY interrupt assert or deassert kills the host with the SAME exit status as the value guards under test.  Any lcabadval row must match the fatal() TEXT INCLUDING ITS OWN DATUM, never the exit status -- an exit-status oracle there cannot tell the defect from the confound.",
