regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\pass1_20260910\codex.txt:3851:.\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28959:   "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time.\n\n*** THE PREREQUISITE IS DISCHARGED.  `#506` LANDED THIS AFTERNOON. ***\n\nThis row's HOLD-Q asked: *\"given that every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit\nstatus, and given that `lcaconfwidth` means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the\ntext-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix land FIRST, making it a\nprerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?\"*\n\n**It landed.**  `#506` repaired both narrowing sites in `dev_lca.c` -- `:205` (IOC_CONF) and the\n`default:` arm -- to `0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, matching the five siblings.  Measured by\n`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`: **HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6, same script**, with `kill width` now reading\n`0x100000000` instead of `0x0`.\n\n> **So the text oracle is now sound at the IOC_CONF arm, and the ordering question in the HOLD-Q is\n> answered by events rather than by adjudication.**  What remains of that HOLD-Q is its SECOND half,\n> untouched: *the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier\n> census dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1` -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.*\n\n*The interrupt-stub confound is also unchanged and still governs the witness design:\n`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal()`+`exit(1)` with the ISA PIC\nchain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the same status as the value guards under\ntest.  That is why the rows must match text, which is why `#506` was the prerequisite.*\n\n*** THE HOLD-Q'S SECOND HALF IS ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX. ***\nEnumerated by reading `if (idata` in `dev_lca.c`, 2026-09-07:\n\n    :203  IOC_CONF   idata & ~1\n    :231  TB_ENA     idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN\n    :246  W_BASE0    idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL\n    :261  W_MASK0    idata != 0x700000ULL\n    :285  W_BASE1    idata != 0x240000000ULL\n    :300  W_MASK1    idata != 0x3ff00000ULL\n\n**Six, not four** -- the earlier census in this row dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1`, exactly as the\nHOLD-Q suspected.  *Note two shapes, which may matter for the fix design: two BITMASK tests\n(`& ~mask`) and four EQUALITY tests, one of which accepts two values.*\n\n**And the eleven-live count is confirmed rather than carried:** `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'`\ngives **12**, of which `:129` sits inside a commented-out block -- so 11 live, in **FIVE** classes:\n2 interrupt stubs, 1 bus/cycle-type, 1 range/lane, 6 value, 1 default.\n\n> #### *** THIS SENTENCE SAID \"four classes\" AND THEN LISTED FIVE.  Corrected 2026-09-07 after the\n> #### grok design pass named it -- and the same slip was in the brief that asked it to. ***\n> The opener asks seats to check enumerations; mine was wrong in its own headline, with the\n> refutation printed three words later.  *A count and the list beside it are two claims, and\n> nothing checks them against each other.*\n\n*** AND THE FIRST HALF OF THE HOLD-Q IS DISCHARGED BY EVENTS: `#506` LANDED (`7a41b3f`). ***\nThe question was whether `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix had to land first, since this row's rows must\nmatch the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit status (the two interrupt stubs kill with the same\nstatus as every value guard).  **It landed and is measured** -- both narrowing sites now print\n`0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6 -- so the IOC_CONF arm's text is sound.\n\n**Design brief out to grok** (`regress/artifacts/lcabadval/DESIGN_brief.md`) rather than codex: the\ncodex seat refused the sibling `lcaconfwidth` census on its content filter, and a brief enumerating\neleven host kills is the same recorded trigger shape.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07: **NOT RATIFY** as a six-armed one-design round until two of my\nclaims are dropped.  The ROUND SHAPE survives; two warrants and one count do not. ***\n\n**1. \"THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX\" STILL UNDER-COUNTS.**  De-escalation owes a row per PREDICATE, but\nthree of the six predicates accept TWO values:\n\n    IOC_CONF  {0, 1}          TB_ENA   {0, 0x80}      W_BASE0  {0, 0x300800000}\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}      W_BASE1  {0x240000000}  W_MASK1  {0x3ff00000}\n\n**Nine pinning values, not six rows** -- and three of the nine are the reset `0`, which is vacuous\nunless sequenced after a nonzero write.  *So replacing the old \"four\" with \"six\" corrected one\nunder-count into another.*\n\n**2. S5 DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND CITING IT WOULD CITE A DEFECT AS A WARRANT.**  I offered the live\n`dev = 0` path (`dev >= 21`, kill commented out) as this file's own *delete-the-kill, substitute-a-\nsafe-default* precedent.  **It is PCI type-0 TAG DECODE, not a register-value policy**: it aliases\n\"no device selected\" onto SLOT 0, which is empty today only because the installed SIO is device 7.\n**And that loop is already filed as [[lcashiftub]] -- `0x800 << 20` in a signed `int` at `dev == 20`\nis UB.**  Substituting `0x700000` when firmware wrote `IOC_W_MASK_1M` (0) would be that same alias\npointed at a documented encoding.\n\n> **THE RIGHT IN-FILE WARRANT IS `W_T_BASE0` / `W_T_BASE1`**: the sibling TRANSLATED-window registers\n> already store any value and do not kill.  *Base/mask killing on a firmware snapshot while\n> translated-base accepts everything is the file's own outlier* -- which is a far stronger warrant\n> than the one I proposed, and one grep away.\n\n**3. THE ROUND SHAPE IS RATIFIED: ONE ROUND, MEMBER 4 ACCEPT, ALL SIX.**  Delete `fatal`+`exit` and\nkeep the store that already ran.  Five of the six fields are pure storage (`window_base_*`,\n`window_mask_*`, `tlb_enable` appear only as `odata = d->X` / `d->X = idata`); the sixth, `ioc_conf`,\nis consumed only as `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, **so extra CONF bits\nare already discarded at the only consumer** and a masked store would be an unlicensed addition.\n**Bitmask vs equality is a DETECTOR-SHAPE split, not a fix-design split.**  FAULT is unavailable on\nAlpha.\n\n*** 4. THE FLOOR-(a) TRAP, and it is the finding that most changes the detector: AN ACCEPT-ONLY\nDETECTOR IS GREEN ON HEAD. ***  Writing the six magic constants SURVIVES on the committed build.  So\na detector that writes only currently-legal values **is green under verbatim reinstatement of all six\noriginal guards** -- the vacuity-floor miss in its purest form.  **The rows that make (b) bite are the\ndocumented-REJECTED writes**: MASK0 `0`, BASE1 with WEN clear, CONF bit 32, a non-TEN TB_ENA bit.\n\n**5. THE (d) FORMS HERE ARE SITE OMISSION AND DISJUNCT-DROPPING, not an operand swap.**  Leave\n`W_MASK1` (or `W_BASE1`) fatal while de-fatalising the other five -- incomplete clause (b), and\n**`ioc rt` in `lca_confwidth_probe.py` stays green because nothing in that probe touches MASK1.**\nSecond form: on `W_BASE0`, drop `idata != 0ULL &&` and keep the magic comparison -- the magic accept\nrow stays green, *which is exactly why BASE0 owes TWO pins*.\n\n**6. `lca_confwidth_probe.py`'s `kill fired` and `died` WANTS WILL INVERT IF THIS ROUND SHIPS.**  It\nis a `#506` witness, not this round's detector.  **Re-pin it under the oracle-correction rule -- do\nnot launder by editing wants after the product change.**\n\n**7. THE STUBS ARE A SIBLING, NOT A PREREQUISITE.**  Construction does not fire them today (PIC\n`ier`/`irr` start 0, 8253 `in_use=0`, WDC only with an IDE image, ns16550 needs `com_ier` bits), and\n`#506`'s legal session already survived on this machine.  **Text is sufficient to ATTRIBUTE a kill\nand is NOT a survival oracle** -- an accept row wanting `alive` still false-reds if a stub fires, and\nthat is controlled by SESSION SHAPE (cold prompt, `step`, don't touch the ISA window), not by\nde-fatalising the stubs first.  *A BOOT witness would make them a prerequisite, because `timer_stop`\nis why stepping does not tick the PIT.*\n\n**8. AND AN HONESTY CONSTRAINT FOR THE CONTRACT:** `default:` still kills `STAT0`/`STAT1`, which the\nheader documents as R/W1C.  **This round can be green and firmware can still die**, so the contract\nmust name that as out of scope or the round will be reported as a false pass of *\"LCA no longer\nhost-kills on IOC MMIO\"*.\n\n**Split out, each its own round:** the interrupt stubs (member 7); `bus != 0` -- *deleting that\n`exit(1)` leaves `dev` UNINITIALISED in the `else`*, which is the sketch rule and not a rider;\n`ofs > len` (already [[lcaisalane]]; `ofs` is address bits 5-6); and the `default:` arm.  **HAE at\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE` already silently ignores writes to a documented register -- that is member 2, not\n4, and must not be unified with the six.**\n\n*** 2026-09-07, CODEX FIX-CHECK: QUALIFIED YES, AND IT FOUND A COUNTEREXAMPLE TO THE ROUND'S CENTRAL WARRANT. ***  All six stores confirmed prior to their guards (table, full denominator); the consumer analysis confirmed with one sharpening -- masking CONF would change READBACK policy, not merely bus selection.  **But `dev_sh4.c's RCR1 comment records that its store USED TO run before the exit and was DELIBERATELY MOVED BELOW THE GUARD**, so \"the store already ran\" is not a general warrant in this tree.  The round now carries an open A-vs-B design question (keep the store vs decline the write) that the W_T_BASE* siblings CANNOT settle, since accepting-anything there may be an absent guard rather than a decision.  Disposition set also grew by two source-backed members (accept/decline WITH REPEATED announcements; complete with a GUEST-VISIBLE DEVICE ERROR, dev_wdc.c's WDCE_ABRT+interrupt).  And a correction to my own brief: only TWO BITS IN STAT0 are R/W1C, not STAT1.\n\n*** TITLE CORRECTED 2026-09-07: it said FOUR registers and the true count is SIX. *** Derived, not recalled: `exit(1)` occurs SEVEN times in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) -- six guarded value-arms (LCA_IOC_CONF :215, TB_ENA :235, W_BASE0 :250, W_MASK0 :265, W_BASE1 :289, W_MASK1 :304) and the `default:` arm at :324, which this round does NOT close.  The four window registers are presumably what the row was opened on; CONF and TB_ENA carry the same shape and were folded in later without the title following.  **A stale title is how a queue row is READ** -- the brief that goes to the panel says six, and a reader comparing the two would have had to guess which was right.  W_T_BASE0 (:272) and W_T_BASE1 (:311) confirmed UNGUARDED: plain readback/store, no value test -- which is the sibling warrant the design brief puts to the panel.\n\n*** AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS ITSELF CORRUPTED WHEN FIRST WRITTEN -- BY THE SHELL, QUIETLY\nENOUGH TO SURVIVE REVIEW. ***  It was applied through `python -c \"...\"` with DOUBLE quotes, and\nbash performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on the backticks inside it: `exit(1)` and `default:` were\nEXECUTED and replaced by their empty output, leaving readable prose with two words silently\nmissing -- \"Derived, not recalled:  occurs SEVEN times\".  bash did print two errors, but python\nran on the mangled source anyway and the write SUCCEEDED, so the record was wrong while the\ncommand reported success.\n\n**Inside a double-quoted shell argument a backtick is a COMMAND, not a quote mark.**  That is the\ncarrier's backslash-through-heredoc class arriving on a new character, and with the same\nsignature: the failure lands in the FILE, not in the exit status.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR FAILED TWICE MORE BEFORE IT LANDED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  The\nfix was retried as `python -c '...'` with SINGLE quotes -- correct for backticks -- and bash\nrejected it, because the note explaining the hazard contains the word \"carrier's\", whose\nAPOSTROPHE terminated the shell quote.  So: double quotes lose to backticks, single quotes lose to\napostrophes, and English prose about shell quoting reliably contains both.\n\n**The rule is not a better quoting style.  It is: PROSE DESTINED FOR A RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT\nFILE, never through `-c`.**  This paragraph was written that way and landed intact on the first\nattempt.\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN PANEL 2026-09-07 (preserved: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/ -- _scratchpad is never committed).  PROVISIONAL: FIVE OF THE\nLANDED SEATS ARE READ HERE AND **CODEX WAS STILL STREAMING** WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN (its file grew\n14,943 -> 30,594 bytes between two reads and its tail is mid-`exec`, not a verdict).  Any ruling\nshort of the roster is provisional on its face, so no disposition is adopted below. ***\n\nSEATS: grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (5); codex IN FLIGHT;\nthe kimi CLI WALLED on an explicit 403 weekly limit, so that seat is COVERED, not lost; agy\nDELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED,\nreserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE PANEL'S DOMINANT FINDING IS THAT MY BRIEF POSED THE WRONG QUESTION, AND FOUR SEATS REACHED\nIT INDEPENDENTLY: THE FORK IS MIS-POSED AS ONE DISPOSITION FOR ALL SIX ARMS. ***\n\nThe kimi substitute put it sharpest, and it is a self-inflicted wound: *\"The fork as posed (one\nanswer for the file) repeats the exact error the #447 census inlines a warning against.\"*  **My\nbrief QUOTED `#447`'s own sentence -- \"Saying 'the store is upstream' once for the file would have\nbeen wrong three times out of four.  Check it per site.\" -- and then asked for a single answer for\nsix arms.**  The warning was inlined as a settled premise and violated in the questions below it.\n\n**WHERE THE SEATS AGREE (4 of 5):** the five PURE-STORAGE arms and `LCA_IOC_CONF` must be decided\nseparately, because CONF is the only one with a consumer (`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`).  For the five,\npremise 6 survives -- grok's formulation is the one to keep: **\"B copies RCR1's *edit* without\nRCR1's *reason*.\"**  RCR1's warrant was that the stored value has a meaning the model otherwise\nhonours; for the five window/TB_ENA fields nothing honours anything, so the edit does not transfer.\nThe kimi substitute sharpens even the agreement: the right analogy for the five is not \"the lie is\npre-existing\" but **`#447`'s ICR cell -- *no honored state to corrupt*** -- which is a stronger and\ndifferently-grounded warrant than the one my premise 6 offered.\n\n**WHERE THEY SPLIT, AND IT IS A REAL DISPUTE TO SETTLE BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY COUNT:**\n\n    CONF -> B                 glm       \"premise 2 names a live consumer, so CONF is exactly RCR1-shaped\"\n    CONF -> A, more so        minimax   \"the consumer uses only bit 0, so a rejected value leaves it\n                                         correct -- and B can SILENTLY BREAK it if the retained old\n                                         value differs in bit 0\"\n    CONF -> decline or mask   kimi      split per arm\n    CONF -> neither           grok      \"treat it as a bit-mask leftover, not a window\"\n\n**minimax's objection to B is the one with a measurable consequence** and it is the opposite of\nglm's: under B a rejected CONF write leaves the PREVIOUS value in place, and if the previous value\ndiffers in bit 0 the PCI bus selection silently changes -- so B is not merely conservative there, it\ncan alter a consumer that A leaves correct.  That is checkable and should be checked before either\nis adopted.\n\n**THE DISSENT, recorded as a dissent and not as noise:** deepseek alone says premise 6 is unsound\nfor all six -- *\"the guard's accepted-value set is the model's CONTRACT.  Storing a rejected value\nand returning it on readback creates a new false belief that the value is supported, even if\naccepted values are also unimplemented.  The existing lie for accepted values does not license\nexpanding the set of lies.\"*  It also warns the reasoning generalises: it would justify removing any\nstore-before-guard exit anywhere.  **That generalisation objection is the strongest single argument\nagainst A and is not answered by the five-versus-one split**, because it attacks the warrant rather\nthan its scope.\n\n**A BRIEF DEFECT OF MINE, named by glm and correct:** premise 2's field counts were *asserted, not\ninlined* -- I ran the greps and wrote the conclusion instead of the OUTPUT.  This project's own rule\nis that a brief inlines the DERIVER'S OUTPUT rather than a literal, precisely so a packet-fed seat\ncan check it.  glm could not settle five of the six arms from the packet for exactly that reason and\nsaid so honestly rather than ratifying.\n\n**NOT ADOPTED, PENDING codex AND THE ROSTER.**  The next brief is per-arm by construction, carries\nthe deriver output rather than its summary, and puts minimax's silently-broken-consumer prediction\nas a question a measurement can answer.\n\n*** THE grok BODY, READ IN FULL 2026-09-07 -- AND NONE OF THE FOLLOWING WAS IN ITS VERDICT LINE.\nThis is the read-the-body rule paying off on the same day a row was filed about not reading. ***\n\n**1. IT ANSWERS deepseek's DISSENT, which the five/one split could not.**  deepseek's objection was\nthat premise 6 generalises -- it would license removing any store-before-guard exit anywhere.  grok\nscopes it precisely: *\"premise 6 is not the reasoning that lets every store-before-guard site keep\nits store.  It is the reasoning that lets a site keep its store WHEN NO ACCEPTED VALUE OF THAT FIELD\nIS CONSUMED.  That is #447's own per-site rule, not an evasion of it.\"*  The warrant is a per-site\ntest with a checkable predicate, not a blanket.\n\n**2. PREMISE 5 SCORED: \"The comments exist.  The transfer does not.\"**  RCR1 refuses CONSUMED\nFEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED CONSTANTS.  That is the distinction the whole\nfork turned on, and it is sharper than my premise 6 offered.\n\n**3. *** OPTION A OPENS A NEW KILL PATH, AND THIS IS THE ROUND'S SAFETY FINDING. ***  Under A, a CONF\nwrite with extra bits that ALSO sets bit 0 reaches `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0` exit(1) -- *\"newly\nreachable ... today that write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  **A is not purely a reduction; it\nconverts one kill into a different reachable one.**  Any round shipping A must say so, and should\nconsider whether `lca_pci_conf` is in scope with it.\n\n**4. THE FILE'S OWN IDIOM IS A, AND THE GUARDS ARE THE OUTLIERS.**  In the SAME switch:\n`LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` already silently ignore; `W_T_BASE0/1` already accept-and-store; and\nthe `dev >= 21` loop already deletes a kill and substitutes a default.  *\"The value guards are the\noutliers in THIS switch, not the house style.\"*  That is a stronger warrant than anything my brief\noffered, and it is one grep away.\n\n**5. THE ANNOUNCE TEXT WOULD BECOME A WRONG RECORD THE MOMENT A LANDS.**  The current string says\n*\"the only implemented value\"* about a value that **is not implemented**.  The honest once-message is\n*\"window translation / extra CONF bits not implemented\"*, not *\"illegal encoding\"*.  Rewrite it or\ndelete it with the exit -- do not carry it forward.\n\n**6. OPTION-SET CORRECTIONS.**  `#443`'s restore is *\"B wearing extra lines\"* -- observationally B\nfor readback, since nothing re-enters between the store and the `break`; not a distinct member.\n**STAT0 is the architecturally correct error path for LCA** (`IOC_STAT0_ERR` is R/W1C) and is\n**BLOCKED**: STAT0/STAT1 have no case and hit `default:` exit(1) -- *\"You cannot report an error\nthrough a register that currently kills the host.\"*  That member owes a STAT0 implementation first,\nwhich is a different round.  **FAULT confirmed unavailable**: `memory_rw.c` raises on `res <= 0` only\nunder MEM_MIPS and MEM_M88K; Alpha compiles with MEM_ALPHA and has no arm, so `return 0` fails the\naccess with no guest exception.\n\n**7. MASKING CONF IS UNLICENSED, WITH A REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THERE IS NO DATASHEET.**  No 21066\ndocument is in the packet or in the tracked `_scratchpad/*.pdf` list, so RAZ/WI versus store-full is\n**not settleable from this tree**.  Premise 3 stands and masking would be an addition without a\nwarrant.\n\n*** 8. THE WITNESS IS NOT PENDING -- THE RECIPE EXISTS.  This moves the round off documented-only. ***\n* **Machine: `alphabook1` ONLY.**  `machine_alpha.c`'s `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` is under\n  `ST_ALPHABOOK1` and no other subtype.\n* **Addresses** (K0SEG | physical, because `alpha_translate_v2p` identity-maps K0SEG in kernel mode):\n  CONF `0xfffffc0180000020`, TB_ENA `...00a0`, BASE0 `...0100`, BASE1 `...0120`, MASK0 `...0140`,\n  MASK1 `...0160`.\n* **`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY issues a guest `stq`/`ldq` through this exact decode at\n  CONF on unmodified `alphabook1`**; the other five are further cases of the same DEVICE_ACCESS on\n  the same mapping.\n* **Three traps that file already paid for, so a new probe need not rediscover them:** debugger\n  `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger `put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is\n  NOT a guest instruction (so it does not witness); and HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so\n  **a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`**.\n\n**9. THE KILL CENSUS FOR THE WHOLE FILE:** eleven live `exit(1)` today; **A removes six; five\nremain** -- the `default:` arm (both reads AND writes, covering STAT0/STAT1 and the rest of the\n512 MB IOC window), `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0`, `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` (filed as [[lcaisalane]]),\nand the bare `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` TODO pair (member 7, not this round).  Residuals not\nof this class: HAE writes discarded with no storage; TBIA ignored; `default:` still narrows\n`relative_addr` through `(int)` -- **`#506` fixed the `idata` half only**; and [[lcashiftub]].\n\n**10. A CENSUS ERROR IN THIS ROW'S OWN HISTORY:** the guards are TWO predicate shapes, not one --\nbit-masks (CONF, TB_ENA) and equality whitelists (the four windows) -- **and `W_BASE0` accepts TWO\nvalues**, so the row's original \"four registers accept exactly one value each\" framing was false of\n`BASE0` before the title was even corrected to six.  One DESIGN still covers all six (delete the\nexit, keep the store, optional latch); one design does NOT if you pick B, because *\"B is a coherent\nanswer only for a consumed field, which five of these are not.\"*\n\n*** THE codex SEAT LANDED LAST AND OVERTURNED THE PANEL ON A FACT CHECKABLE IN ONE GREP.  MY\nPREMISE 6 IS DEAD, AND SO IS THE FORMULATION I HAD CREDITED grok WITH. ***\n\n**codex's verdict:** *\"Your argument for A fails as written: RCR1 also has no functional consumer.\nI recommend B for pass 1 as a conservative policy choice; hardware-correct write/readback behavior\nremains unproved.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED HERE, not taken on trust** -- `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` returns exactly FOUR occurrences:\n\n    dev_sh4.c:388    uint8_t   rtc_rcr1;              declaration\n    dev_sh4.c:2776   odata = d->rtc_rcr1;             READBACK\n    dev_sh4.c:2781   ...landed in rtc_rcr1 and...     INSIDE THE COMMENT\n    dev_sh4.c:2805   d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;             STORE\n\n**Zero functional consumers.**  And the comparison that settles it: `ioc_conf` also has four\noccurrences, but its fourth is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` -- a real consumer.  **So RCR1 is consumed\nLESS than LCA's CONF, not more.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS DESTROYS. ***  My premise 6 claimed LCA differs from RCR1 because *\"RCR1's stored value\nhas a meaning the model otherwise honours\"*.  It has no such meaning: the model stores it and reads\nit back, exactly like the five LCA window fields.  **grok's formulation, which I recorded above as\n\"the keeper\" -- *\"RCR1 refuses CONSUMED FEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED\nCONSTANTS\"* -- is FALSE IN ITS FIRST HALF.**  So is my own gloss that `#447`'s edit \"does not\ntransfer\".\n\n**IT TRANSFERS EXACTLY.**  `#447` faced a store-before-guard field with NO functional consumer,\nand chose to MOVE THE STORE BELOW THE GUARD.  That is the LCA shape, arm for arm.  The tree's own\nprecedent is therefore evidence FOR B, which is what codex, deepseek and (for CONF) glm all said.\n\n*** AND THE SURVIVING ARGUMENT IS THE ONE deepseek RAISED FIRST AND codex SHARPENED: THE GUEST IS\nITSELF A CONSUMER OF READBACK. ***  codex: *\"Previously, a rejected write ended execution before\nsubsequent guest code could observe that value.  A creates that observation and allows the guest to\nmake further decisions from it.  Existing incomplete behavior for accepted settings does not\nestablish that these newly admitted executions are equally harmless.\"*  That argument is untouched by\nthe consumer census, because it does not depend on a MODEL consumer at all -- and it is precisely\nwhat `#447`'s comment means by *\"the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC\nwill interrupt it.\"*\n\n**TALLY, and the argument decides it rather than the count:** codex -> B; deepseek -> B; glm -> B for\nCONF and not settleable for the five; kimi substitute -> split per arm; grok -> A; minimax -> A.  But\ngrok's and minimax's A both rest on the consumed/unconsumed distinction, **and that distinction is\nnow measured false.**  Count the arguments, not the seats: the load-bearing premise under the A\nposition has been refuted by a grep.\n\n**THIS IS THE THIRD REVERSAL IN THIS ROUND'S DESIGN AND IT COST NOTHING, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE\nHOLD DISCIPLINE.**  No code is committed; the round has produced adjudication text and zero product\nchanges.  The earlier records above -- premise 6 \"sound for five arms\", grok's formulation as \"the\nkeeper\" -- are left standing WITH this correction beneath them rather than silently edited, because\na future reader needs to see that the panel's majority reasoning was overturned and by what.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOW OWED BEFORE ANY FIX: the ARM-BY-ARM design, with the RCR1 precedent read as\nSUPPORTING B, and codex's readback-consumer argument answered rather than assumed away.  The\nquestion that remains genuinely open is minimax's measurable one -- under B a rejected CONF write\nretains the previous value, and codex adds that W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 RESET TO ZERO, which is NOT\nin their accepted set, so \"retain the last accepted value\" is not even well-defined at reset.  That\nis a concrete defect in B as stated and it needs an answer (retain? force the reset value? decline\nonly the guarded bits?) before B can ship. ***\n\n*** AND THE SHARPEST LESSON IS NOT THE REVERSAL, IT IS HOW MY GLOSS BEAT MY OWN PRIMARY SOURCE. ***\n\nThe brief inlined #447s RCR1 comment VERBATIM.  That comment argues purely from READBACK:\n\n    the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC will\n    interrupt it.  It never will.\n\n**It never claims a model consumer.**  The consumed/unconsumed framing is MINE -- premise 6 glossed\nit as RCR1s stored value having a meaning the model otherwise honours -- and grok adopted my\ngloss rather than the quoted text sitting directly above it, producing B copies RCR1s edit\nwithout RCR1s reason.  codex went to the source, grepped the field, and found the gloss false.\n\n> **INLINING THE PRIMARY SOURCE DOES NOT PROTECT A BRIEF FROM A WRONG SUMMARY PLACED BESIDE IT.**\n> A seat reads the summary as the QUESTION and the quote as SUPPORTING MATERIAL, so a false gloss\n> steers the answer even when the refuting text is on the same page.  This projects premise-audit\n> rule says to state load-bearing premises AS QUESTIONS; the refinement is that a premise which\n> RESTATES an inlined quotation is the most dangerous kind, because it looks already verified.\n\n*The cheap defence is the one codex used and I did not: when a premise characterises what some\nother code does, GREP THAT CODE.  `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` is four lines of output and it was\navailable to me before the brief went out.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*\n\n*** THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER IS NAMED, AND THE TWO-OPTION FRAMING WAS REALLY A ONE-OPTION\nFRAMING.  codex, 2026-09-08; the two load-bearing claims VERIFIED here before recording. ***\n\n**VERDICT: C -- decline the out-of-set write, PRESERVE the previous register value, announce once.**\n\n### 1.  A AND B OVERLAP.  There was never a choice between them.\n\n*\"Your reconstructed A and B overlap: both store the written value and continue.  With the existing\nreads retained, B describes SHADOW STORAGE WITH READBACK, not a write-only register.  It supplies no\ndistinct state-transition policy.\"*  **So the panel that argued A-versus-B was arguing about one\noption under two names** -- the option-set-completeness failure in its purest form, and this row had\nit for two rounds.\n\n### 2.  *** WHAT `#447` ACTUALLY DOES IS DECLINE-AND-PRESERVE, AND I HAD IT BACKWARDS. ***\n\nThe precedent was cited as \"a register with no consumer, where the write is accepted\".  The\nimplementation says otherwise -- **the store is in the ELSE branch**:\n\n    if (idata & (SH_RCR1_CIE | SH_RCR1_AIE)) {\n            /* bounded diagnostic */\n    } else\n            d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;\n\n*\"Unsupported writes preserve the previous value.  They are NOT accepted into backing storage.\"*  And\nthe reason recorded in that comment is a READBACK concern -- echoing interrupt-enable flags would\nsuggest functionality the model does not provide -- so **absence of a consumer neither disqualifies\ndecline-and-preserve nor argues for accepting everything**: readback is guest-observable on its own.\n\n### 3.  *** `ioc_conf` HAS A FUNCTIONAL CONSUMER.  VERIFIED. ***\n\n`dev_lca.c:121` is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;`, feeding the PCI decode.  **The row's \"no functional\nconsumer\" premise is false for CONF**, whatever it is for the other five.\n\n> **AND IT TIES TWO HIGH ROWS TOGETHER.**  `lcaconfbus1kill` is precisely about that consumer: a\n> write of `0x1` makes `bus == 1` and arms the `bus != 0` host kill.  `0x1` is INSIDE this arm's\n> accepted set `{0, 1}`, so **C does not protect against it and must not be described as if it\n> does.**  The two rows are separate defects on one register and both need fixing.\n\n### 4.  THE RESET QUESTION IS DISSOLVED, NOT ANSWERED -- and the reason improves the fix.\n\n*\"The predicates govern WRITES, not all possible stored states.\"*  Initialization assigns zero with\nno guard; reads return the stored value with no guard; **and the write assigns BEFORE the guard is\nevaluated.**  So the model can return zero at reset while refusing a later write of zero:\n*\"that asymmetry is established; it is not a logical contradiction.\"*\n\n    register  writes the guard accepts        reset 0 passes?\n    CONF      {0, 1}                          yes\n    TB_ENA    {0, 0x80}                       yes\n    W_BASE0   {0, 0x300800000}                yes\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}                      NO\n    W_BASE1   {0x240000000}                   NO\n    W_MASK1   {0x3ff00000}                    NO\n\n*** MY OWN ADDITION, from verifying the store-order claim at `:233` vs the guard at `:236`: BECAUSE\nTHE STORE HAPPENS FIRST, SIMPLY DELETING THE `exit(1)` LEAVES THE BAD VALUE STORED. ***  That is not\na weaker version of C -- it is a DIFFERENT OBSERVABLE STATE, readable by the guest through the\nexisting read arm.  **C requires moving each store behind its guard, exactly as RCR1 does.**\n\n### 5.  THE SIBLING EVIDENCE SUPPORTS BOTH POLICIES AND SELECTS NEITHER\n\nInside this same switch:\n\n    LCA_IOC_BASE, LCA_IOC_TBIA      discard writes; reads return 0; success\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1             store writes and return them on reads\n    default:                        diagnose and TERMINATE, no backing storage\n\n*\"Thus the sibling evidence supports BOTH discarding writes and retaining shadow state.  It does not\nselect a universal policy.\"*  **A useful negative result: the grep-the-fork rule found the member but\ncannot settle the policy here**, because the file is internally inconsistent.\n\n*`default:` is explicitly OUT of this round -- it has no previous value to preserve, so C is not even\nexpressible there.*\n\n### 6.  THE CENSUS SURVIVES ITS AUDIT\n\nEleven live explicit `exit(1)`: the six value arms at **248, 268, 283, 298, 322, 337**, plus\n**73, 85, 167, 196, 357**.  A bare `exit(` grep returns thirteen; two are comments (the explanatory\nmention and the disabled call whose live fallback is `dev = 0`).  *This is the row's third census and\nthe first to survive.\n\n*** CORRECTION, WITHIN THE HOUR AND BEFORE THIS PROPAGATED: \"THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER\" IS WRONG.\nTHERE IS NO THIRD MEMBER.  THE OPTION SET WAS ALREADY RIGHT AND **MY BRIEF CORRUPTED IT**. ***\n\n`tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md` §7i states the fork exactly as the earlier panel left it:\n\n    A  delete fatal()+exit(1) from all six arms, KEEP the store that already ran\n    B  DECLINE -- move the store BELOW the guard, as #447 did for SH4's RCR1\n\n**The queue's B IS codex's C.**  Decline-and-preserve was already ON the table, correctly described,\nwith the `#447` precedent already attached to it.  My `DISPOSITION2_brief.md` reconstructed B as\n*\"treat the register as write-only backing state\"* -- which is NOT what B says -- and that\nreconstruction is what made A and B overlap.\n\n> **SO THE OPTION-SET FAILURE WAS IN THE BRIEF, NOT IN THE ROW.**  The row had two genuinely\n> distinct members; the brief collapsed them into one and then asked the panel for a third.  *A\n> brief that mis-states the options gets a panel arguing about the wrong set* -- which is the\n> option-set rule's own warning, arriving from the direction it does not usually name: not a MISSING\n> member, an ALTERED one.\n\n**codex flagged this itself and I read past it:** *\"If the earlier panel called decline-and-preserve\n'B,' its letter assignment differs from your reconstruction.  The quoted verdict alone cannot\nresolve that historical naming.  Its implemented precedent is unambiguous.\"*  **It hedged the exact\nerror, derived the right disposition anyway from the `#447` source, and I recorded its answer under a\nletter that does not exist in this row's own queue entry.**\n\n*** WHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION, and it is nearly all of it: ***\n\n* **The DISPOSITION is unchanged and now has two independent derivations** -- the earlier panel\n  reached decline-and-preserve as B, and codex reached it from `#447`'s implementation without\n  trusting my framing.\n* **The `#447` reading is new and load-bearing**: the store sits in the ELSE branch, so unsupported\n  writes PRESERVE the previous value.  *The brief had that backwards.*\n* **`ioc_conf`'s consumer at `dev_lca.c:121` is new**, and it ties this row to `lcaconfbus1kill`.\n* **The store-before-guard order at `:233`/`:236` is new**, and it is what makes A and B differ in\n  OBSERVABLE STATE rather than only in tidiness.\n* **The reset-coherence dissolution and the accepted-set table are new.**\n* **The census survived a third audit.**\n\n**What does NOT survive is the sentence \"the third option-set member is named\".**  Nothing was\nmissing.  *Recorded in place rather than deleted, because a correction that hides what it corrects\nis how this row got to three reversals in the first place.*\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** THE 21066 MANUAL IS LOCAL NOW, AND IT PUTS A NUMBER ON THIS ROW FOR THE FIRST TIME -- BUT THE\nNUMBER IS MY DERIVATION AND IS OUT WITH A PANEL, NOT ESTABLISHED. ***\n\n**QUOTED, `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9572-9578`, read locally:**\n\n> *\"The window mask register IOC_W_MASK provides a mask corresponding to bits <31:20> of an\n> incoming PCI address.  The size of each window is programmed to be from 1 MB to 4 GB ...  Table\n> 6-6 shows the supported values of IOC_W_MASK register bits <31:20>; **other values are\n> unsupported**.\"*\n\n**Table 6-6 (`:9581-9598`) has THIRTEEN rows** -- a doubling run from 1 MB (`0x000`) to 4 GB\n(`0xfff`), each a contiguous run of low ones.\n\n**MY DERIVATION, flagged as mine:** the register value is the field shifted left 20, so the fork's\ntwo accepted constants are\n\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0   accepts ONLY 0x700000     ->  field 0x007  ->    8 MB   legal, 1 of 13\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK1   accepts ONLY 0x3ff00000   ->  field 0x3ff  ->    1 GB   legal, 1 of 13\n\nand the legal set is exactly `(f & (f + 1)) == 0`, verified over all thirteen.\n\n> **IF THAT IS RIGHT, the model terminates the host on TWELVE DOCUMENTED-LEGAL VALUES PER MASK\n> REGISTER -- 24 across the two -- and a guest programming any window size other than 8 MB / 1 GB\n> is doing something the hardware manual explicitly supports.**  That is a much sharper severity\n> statement than *\"rejects values outside a small accepted set\"*, because it names the denominator.\n\n*** IT IS NOT RECORDED AS ESTABLISHED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. ***  The load-bearing step is my own\narithmetic, done in one sitting, on a table extracted by `pdftotext` -- and this project's rule is\nthat an extracted table can stagger names against values.  **Table 6-6 LOOKS self-calibrating (a\ndoubling bit-run beside a doubling size run, thirteen against thirteen), but the calibration rule\nsays one point is only valid for a UNIFORM stagger and you cannot tell which kind you have from one\npoint.**  So it went out to a panel with *\"check the premises, not just the derivations\"* and with\nthe derivation shown step by step:\n\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/MANUAL_brief.md   file-reading seats: codex, grok -- also asked to\n                                                  read S6.4 for the OTHER FOUR arms and to give a\n                                                  count WITH ITS DENOMINATOR per register\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/PACKET_brief.md   packet-fed seats: glm, deepseek, minimax, kimi --\n                                                  Table 6-6 inlined verbatim so they can check the\n                                                  arithmetic without file access\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each explicitly:** the manual enumerates legal values\nfor W_MASK and (as far as I read) NOT for W_BASE, so the two registers may need different\ndispositions; `0x300800000` and `0x240000000` are 34-bit values and I have not established how wide\nthese registers are or what bits above 31 mean; and the more dangerous error direction is a real\nquestion -- terminating on a legal value, versus accepting a value the model's own address decode\nthen does not honour.\n\n*** THE PANEL ANSWERED: SIX SEATS, MY COUNT CONFIRMED -- AND THE ONE SEAT THAT DISSENTED MADE THE\nDEFECT BIGGER, NOT SMALLER. ***\n\n    codex     CONFIRMS 12/13 per register, 24/26 register-value pairs, and Table 6-6 IS\n              self-calibrating -- it parsed all thirteen rows and checked the adjacent runs\n    glm       \"the S3 arithmetic is right -- I re-derived every step and found no defect\"\n    kimi      \"your derivation is right\": shift, both fields, the count, the invariant\n    minimax   \"the S3 derivation is correct\"\n    grok      answered, 14,818 b\n    deepseek  CORRECT ONLY IF bits <19:0> must be zero -- AND THAT PREMISE IS NOT ESTABLISHED\n              BY THE TEXT I QUOTED\n\n*** deepseek IS RIGHT, AND codex SETTLED IT FROM THE OTHER END. ***  My quote enumerates bits\n<31:20> and says nothing about <19:0>.  codex read the preface: **RES fields are type `X` --\nIGNORED ON WRITES, unpredictable on reads, NOT must-be-zero** (`21066_66a_hrm.txt:955`, `:985`).\n\n> **SO THE GUARD IS WORSE THAN \"ACCEPTS ONE OF THIRTEEN\".  IT TESTS THE WHOLE 64-BIT OPERAND\n> AGAINST A SINGLE LITERAL -- `if (idata != 0x700000ULL)` -- WHILE THE HARDWARE DEFINES A 12-BIT\n> FIELD AND IGNORES THE REST.**  A guest writing `0x7000ff`, functionally identical on real\n> hardware, terminates the emulator.  *deepseek said so in as many words: \"if lower bits are\n> ignored, the number of legal register values is much larger than 13 per mask, and the code is\n> even more restrictive.\"*\n\n**AND IT SUPPLIED THE TRAP A NAIVE FIX WOULD HIT:** the invariant must be applied to the SHIFTED\nFIELD.  `(idata & (idata + 1)) == 0` on the full value **REJECTS THE LEGAL `0x700000`**, because\n`0x700000 & 0x700001 == 0x700000`.  The guard is `f = idata >> 20; (f & (f + 1)) == 0`.\n\n*** FOUR MORE CORRECTIONS FROM codex, EACH NARROWING OR SHARPENING A CLAIM OF MINE. ***\n\n* **MY OWN TABLE DROPPED A ROW.**  `MANUAL_brief.md` reformatted Table 6-6 into three columns and\n  **lost `0000 0001 1111` = 32 MB** -- twelve rows under a sentence saying thirteen.  The PACKET\n  brief has all thirteen.  *A transcription error in the brief that asks others to check my\n  transcription.*\n* **ALIGNMENT AND NON-OVERLAP, which my quote stopped one paragraph short of** (`:9618`, S6.4.9):\n  bases must align naturally to window size and windows must not overlap.  **With the two accepted\n  bases held FIXED, window 0 admits only the four sizes through 8 MB and window 1 the eleven\n  through 1 GB** -- so in that configuration the guards reject 3/4 and 10/11.  All thirteen sizes\n  are supported; changing size can require changing the surrounding configuration.\n* **`IOC_CONF` IS A TWO-BIT FIELD, values 0..3, so `idata & ~1` rejects 2 of 4** defined settings --\n  I had quoted the guard without checking the field width.\n* **`IOC_TB_ENA` IS NOT A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT AT ALL: TEN has exactly two states and BOTH are\n  accepted -- 0/2.**  Its only defect is the ignored-bit one.  *Recorded because a round that\n  \"fixes six arms\" would be overclaiming.*\n\n*** THE SHARPEST SINGLE FINDING, AND IT IS NOT MINE: THE MANUAL REQUIRES INITIALISING BOTH BASE\nREGISTERS TO ZERO (S6.4.14, `:10788`), AND `LCA_IOC_W_BASE1` REJECTS EXACTLY THAT. ***  A guest\nfollowing the manual own documented initialisation sequence terminates the emulator.  That is not a\ncorner value -- it is the prescribed setup.\n\n**Also established:** the registers are **64-bit, quadword access only** (S6.4 excludes longword,\n`:10175`); and **all six arms STORE THE VALUE BEFORE TESTING IT** (`d->window_mask_0 = idata;`\nprecedes the guard), so device state is already updated when the process dies.\n\n**DISPOSITION IS NOT SETTLED HERE.**  deepseek proposes accept-with-backing-storage for the values\nthe manual or existing code already treats as legal, plus a `(once)` decline otherwise, and warns\nagainst expanding the W_BASE set without evidence since the manual enumerates none.  That is a\ndesign question for the round, and the round must not silently widen an accept set.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS IN, AND IT QUALIFIES THREE OF THE ASSESS PANEL'S FINDINGS -- ONE OF THEM A\nCONCLUSION I HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN. ***\n\n**VERDICT (codex):** *preserve masked register state; validate `W_MASK` FIELDS; remove the\n`W_BASE` magic-value whitelists; leave TEN's two-state policy alone but correct `TB_ENA`'s\nreserved-bit guard.*  **\"This fixes register handling, not DMA translation\"** -- the scope\nsentence the round must carry.\n\n* *** `TB_ENA` IS NOT EXEMPT AFTER ALL, AND MY RECORD SAID IT WAS. ***  I recorded *\"IOC_TB_ENA is\n  NOT a functional defect at all -- TEN has exactly two states and both are accepted (0/2)\"*.  That\n  is right **only for normalised TEN values**: the arm's other bits are `RES`, type `X`, so the\n  existing whole-operand guard **still rejects writes the hardware ignores**.  Leaving the arm\n  untouched would contradict the very answer given to Q2.  *A per-field finding restated as a\n  per-arm exemption -- the dropped-qualifier class again.*\n* **ALL SIX REGISTERS ARE WRITE-ONLY, not RW.**  The manual makes reads of IOC write-only registers\n  UNPREDICTABLE, so **preserving readback is a useful existing emulator convention, not a hardware\n  requirement.**  A round that \"accepts with backing storage\" should say which of the two it is\n  doing.\n* **AND A PRECISION CORRECTION TO HOW I STATED THE RES RULE:** *\"`RES` alone does not mean ignored\n  -- the decisive evidence is each register table's type `X`, interpreted through the\n  conventions.\"*  My record leaned on \"RES fields are type X\" as one fact; it is two, and only the\n  second carries the ignored-on-write conclusion.\n* **`IOC_CONF` has four REPRESENTABLE field encodings**, and calling them four *supported PCI\n  operating modes* goes beyond the evidence -- the HRM also defers to the PCI specification's\n  configuration-cycle encoding restrictions.  So the \"2 of 4 rejected\" figure is a count of\n  representable encodings, not of legal modes.\n\n**Table 6-6's thirteen entries and the rest of the numerical premises are re-confirmed.**\n\n*** THE SHARP FINDING IS NOT THE OFFSET COUNT.  IT IS THAT TWO DOCUMENTED REGISTERS HAVE NO CASE,\nAND ONE OF THEM IS THE ERROR-STATUS REGISTER A DRIVER READS AFTER A FAILED PROBE.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nA codex ANALYSIS job (the construction-shaped brief is refused; see [[codexcyberflag]]) enumerated\nthe `lca_ioc' region and its stream ended before a verdict.  Its numbers were then VERIFIED here\nrather than taken:\n\n    memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000, ...)   512 MiB\n    switch (relative_addr + LCA_IOC_BASE)                    NO shift, NO mask before the switch\n    named cases: 10        0x20000000 - 10 = 536,870,902 byte offsets reach the shared `default:'\n\n**That count is arithmetically right and it is the WEAKER half of the finding.**  Most of those\noffsets are not registers at all, and the carrier's own rule says a domain is itself a premise: the\nAlpha alignment gate has not been analysed here, so the count of REACHABLE ACCESSES is smaller than\n536 million by an unmeasured factor.  It is recorded with its deriver and NOT as a severity claim.\n\n*** THE STRONGER HALF, from comparing the HEADER's register list against the switch's cases: ***\n\n    LCA_IOC_HAE        +0x000   HANDLED  (via `case LCA_IOC_BASE:' -- HAE and BASE are the same\n                                          address; see the correction below)\n    LCA_IOC_CONF       +0x020   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_STAT0      +0x040   *** NO CASE ***   Status 0\n    LCA_IOC_STAT1      +0x060   *** NO CASE ***   Status 1\n    LCA_IOC_TBIA       +0x080   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_TB_ENA     +0x0a0   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_BASE0/1  +0x100/0x120   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0/1  +0x140/0x160   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1 +0x180/0x1a0  HANDLED\n\n**TWO of the twelve documented registers have no case, and they are STAT0 and STAT1.**\n\n> *** AND STAT0 IS EXACTLY THE REGISTER THE 21066 MANUAL SAYS RECORDS A FAILED PCI ACCESS.\n> `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt' S6.2.18 (PCI Master Abort): an unanswered read \"returns arbitrary\n> data with error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception\", plus an internal\n> interrupt and ERR/CODE/CMD/ADDR logging -- and that log is STAT0. ***\n>\n> **So the ordinary driver error-recovery sequence -- probe a PCI slot, take a master abort, then\n> read the status register to find out what happened -- reaches `fatal()' + `exit(1)'.**  That is\n> not an exotic value or an unusual address.  It is what a driver does when a probe fails.\n\n*** AND IT PUTS THIS ROW ON THE SAME GUEST CODE PATH AS `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]], WHICH MATTERS\nFOR ORDERING RATHER THAN FOR SCOPE. ***  `#515` makes an absent-device config access return the\nempty-slot signature instead of terminating the host.  A driver that then consults STAT0 to check\nfor the error the manual says accompanies it lands here.  **The two rows are adjacent steps of one\ndriver sequence** -- which is an argument for scheduling this row next, and NOT an argument for\nfolding it into `#515` (different handler, different semantic class, different detector).\n\n*** A CORRECTION TO MY OWN CHECK, made before it could mislead: THE FIRST RUN REPORTED **THREE**\nUNHANDLED REGISTERS AND NAMED `LCA_IOC_HAE' AMONG THEM.  THAT IS WRONG. ***  `alpha_lcareg.h`\ndefines `LCA_IOC_HAE` as `LCA_IOC_BASE` -- the same address -- and the switch carries\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE:`, so HAE is handled under the other name.  **My script matched cases by NAME\nwhen the thing that decides handledness is the VALUE.**\n\n> That is this file's own deriver rule landing on me: *\"BEFORE WRITING A DERIVER, ASK WHAT MAKES A\n> MEMBER A MEMBER.  If the answer is a property the regex does not test, the deriver is measuring a\n> CORRELATE, and a correlate's error is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.\"*\n> A by-name census of a switch whose labels are aliases returns a plausible, wrong answer -- and it\n> inflated the finding by 50% in the direction that would have made the row look worse.\n\n**STILL OPEN and deliberately NOT answered here:** whether the two `default:` arms (read and write)\nare symmetric, what the write side does with `idata`, and what the manual says STAT0's reset value\nand semantics are.  The codex job was cut off before Q3-Q5 and its remaining questions stand.\n\n*** THE codex RE-FIRE LANDED WITH A VERDICT, AND IT REFUTED ONE OF MY OWN PREMISES. 2026-09-08. ***\n(The first firing could not resolve the manual -- a repo-relative path, when `_scratchpad/` sits one\nlevel ABOVE the git root -- and said so plainly instead of guessing.  A brief defect, not a seat\nfailure.  Re-fired with the absolute path.)\n\n**MY Q4 ASKED whether the READ and WRITE `default:` arms are symmetric.  codex: the question has no\nsubject.**\n\n    VERDICT: \"The claimed default-arm asymmetry is false: `lca_ioc' has ONE SHARED terminating\n             `default:'.\"\n\nCHECKED: `awk '/DEVICE_ACCESS\\(lca_ioc\\)/,/^}/' | grep -c 'default:'` = **1**.  I had assumed two\narms because the sibling handler `lca_pci_conf` splits by `writeflag`; this one does not.  *A brief\nthat asks about an asymmetry invites an answer about which side is broader, and the honest answer\nwas that there is one side.*\n\n*** THE COUNT, WITH THE ALIGNMENT QUALIFICATION I HAD NOT ASKED FOR AND WHICH THE CARRIER'S OWN\nDOMAIN RULE DEMANDS. ***\n\n    registration     0x180000000, length 0x20000000 = 536,870,912 bytes, ending 0x19fffffff\n    recognised       10 relative offsets: 0x000 (BASE, also HAE), 0x020, 0x080, 0x0a0,\n                     0x100, 0x120, 0x140, 0x160, 0x180, 0x1a0\n    reach default    536,870,902 byte offsets      (= 0x20000000 - 10)\n    QUADWORD-ALIGNED  67,108,854                   <- the honest figure for ordinary accesses\n\n**codex volunteered the alignment split rather than letting the big number stand**, noting the\nhandler adds the base *without shifting, masking, or checking alignment*, and that the permitted\nfile set could not establish what the dispatcher actually delivers.  It also cites the manual (S6.4)\nrequiring quadword IOC accesses -- so the 67 million figure is the one an ordinary driver can reach,\nand unaligned delivery is not needed to make the point.\n\n*** TEN ACTIVE `exit(1)` CALLS IN THIS ONE FILE, enumerated by codex and consistent with what this\nsession has separately measured: ***\n\n    2   the interrupt callbacks -- UNCONDITIONAL on entry, no predicate at all   -> [[lcairqstubkill]]\n    1   the `lca_isa' guard `ofs > len'\n    6   `lca_ioc' value guards\n    1   the shared `lca_ioc' default\n    ---\n    (plus the one `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]] has just REMOVED from `lca_pci_conf')\n\n*** THE `lca_isa' GUARD IS REACHABLE, BUT ONLY BY A BYTE OR WORD ACCESS -- and codex's phrasing was\ncorrectly qualified where a careless reading of it would not be. ***  It said the manual's\n*\"ordinary longword and quadword accesses cannot satisfy `ofs > len'\"*.  Enumerated here rather than\ninferred, since `ofs = relative_addr & 3` is 0..3:\n\n    len >= 4 (longword, quadword)   ofs > len is UNSATISFIABLE          -- codex is right\n    len == 1 (byte)                 ofs = 2 or 3  ->  REACHABLE\n    len == 2 (word)                 ofs = 3       ->  REACHABLE\n\n**So it is not a dead guard; it is a guard no CONFORMING access reaches.**  Three (ofs, len) pairs\nreach it, all of them sub-longword.  *Worth stating precisely because \"the manual says accesses are\nquadword\" is an argument about what a good driver does, not about what the model will accept -- and\nthe model accepts a byte access and then terminates the host.*\n\n**SCOPE, so this row does not silently swallow its neighbours:** the interrupt callbacks are\n[[lcairqstubkill]] and are separately witnessed; the `lca_isa` guard is a DIFFERENT handler with a\ndifferent reachability story and should be its own row rather than folded in here.  This row remains\nthe six `lca_ioc` value guards plus the shared default."
regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\pass1_20260910\codex.txt:3854:.\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:33489:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  Surfaced while verifying a claim in the grok seat's `lcabadval` pass-1 BODY (its\nverdict line did not carry it) -- and **verified to be sharper than the seat stated it**, by opening\nboth functions.\n\n**THE DEFECT: an ACCEPTED write to one register arms a host kill in a DIFFERENT function.**\n\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)`, `case LCA_IOC_CONF`:\n\n    d->ioc_conf = idata;\n    /*  Only bit 0 is implemented so far, the PCI bus 0 vs\n        bus non-0 selection bit.  */\n    if (idata & ~1) {\n            fatal(\"TODO: Write to unimplemented bit of IOC_CONF: 0x%\" PRIx64 \"\\n\", ...);\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n**`0x1` PASSES that guard.**  `idata & ~1` is 0, so the write is accepted, stored, and the arm's own\ncomment describes bit 0 as the implemented selector.\n\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` then consumes it:\n\n    bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    if (bus == 0) {\n            for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n                    if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                            break;\n            if (dev >= 21) {\n                    /*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n                    exit(1);  */\n                    dev = 0;\n            }\n    } else {\n            fatal(\"TODO. Non-zero bus.\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\nSo: **guest writes 1 to IOC_CONF (accepted), then performs any PCI configuration-space access, and\nthe host process exits.**  Two guest accesses, both to registers the model implements, neither of\nthem rejected.\n\n*** WHY IT IS ITS OWN ROW AND NOT PART OF [[lcabadval]]. ***  Every kill `lcabadval` covers is a\nguard REFUSING a value it does not recognise.  **This one fires on a value the guard ACCEPTS**, in a\ndifferent function, on a later access.  The acceptance and the kill are separated in both code and\ntime, which is why a per-arm audit of `lca_ioc` walks straight past it -- as mine did, twice.\n\n*** AND THE SEAT'S FRAMING NEEDED CORRECTING, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING. ***  grok wrote\nthat this exit becomes *\"newly reachable from a CONF extra-bit write that also sets bit 0; today\nthat write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  That is true of an EXTRA-BIT write (e.g. `0x3`), and it\nmade the kill sound like a CONSEQUENCE of adopting option A.  **It is not: `0x1` alone reaches it\ntoday, with no fix applied.**  A widens which CONF values arrive here; it does not open the door.\nThe door is open now.  *Checked by reading both arms rather than by accepting the seat's sentence --\nwhich is this project's own rule, and the seat was right about the site while being wrong about the\ntiming.*\n\n**THE IRONY, and it is a design signal rather than a joke:** the `bus == 0` branch is the file's own\ngraceful-degradation PRECEDENT -- a commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted --\nand [[lcabadval]] cites that very loop as a transferable in-tree warrant.  **The `else` five lines\nbelow it is a bare kill.**  Same function, opposite dispositions, no comment explaining why.\n\n**REACHABILITY, stated honestly rather than assumed.**  Bit 0 is a bus SELECTOR, so a guest reaches\nthis by selecting a bus the model does not implement and then doing config access -- plausible for\nfirmware or an OS that probes beyond bus 0, and trivially reachable by a probe.  **Whether any\nshipped guest on `alphabook1` actually does it is NOT established here**, and the row does not claim\nit.  What is established is that no guard stands between an accepted write and the kill.\n\n**WITNESS: a rung-3 probe is available and most of it already exists.**  `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`\nis `ST_ALPHABOOK1` ONLY (`machine_alpha.c`), and `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY drives a\nguest `stq`/`ldq` at `LCA_IOC_CONF` (`0xfffffc0180000020`, K0SEG | physical) on unmodified\n`alphabook1`.  This row needs one more session: `stq 0x1` to CONF, then a PCI config-space access.\n**Three traps that probe already paid for:** debugger `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger\n`put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is NOT a guest instruction, so it does not witness; and\nHAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`.\n\n**DISPOSITION, not yet adjudicated:** it is the same de-fatalisation family, but the option set\ndiffers because the kill is not a value refusal -- the honest members here are DECLINE THE CONFIG\nACCESS (return without dispatching, with a latched announce) or IMPLEMENT bus != 0.  **Note the\narchitecturally correct error path is blocked for the same reason grok gave on the sibling row:\n`LCA_IOC_STAT0`/`STAT1` have no case at all and hit the `default:` arm's `exit(1)`, so LCA cannot\nreport a device error through the register the header designates for it.**\n\nsev HIGH: a guest-drivable host kill, reached by two ordinary accesses with no rejected value\nanywhere on the path.\n\n*** SIBLING CHECK DISCHARGED WITH A DENOMINATOR, 2026-09-07 -- the class has EXACTLY ONE member in\nthis file. ***  The rule is grep for the siblings before calling a finding whole; here the question\nis which lca_ioc fields have a consumer at all, since a field nothing reads cannot arm a delayed\nkill.  Counting references that are neither the readback (`odata = d->X`) nor the store (`d->X = `):\n\n    ioc_conf          1 consumer   <- `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in lca_pci_conf.  THIS ROW.\n    tlb_enable        0\n    window_base_0     0            window_base_1     0\n    window_mask_0     0            window_mask_1     0\n    window_t_base_0   0            window_t_base_1   0\n\n**So the accepted-write-arms-a-later-kill shape cannot recur elsewhere in dev_lca.c**: the other\nseven fields are write-and-read-back with nothing downstream.  That is also why [[lcabadval]]s five\npure-storage arms are a different problem from this one, and why a fix here does not generalise to\nthem.\n\n*Not claimed beyond this file.  The general class -- an ACCEPTED store that a later access consumes\ninto a terminating path -- is a plausible cross-device census and is NOT run here.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS-DESIGN CHECK WAS REFUSED BY codex ON A CONTENT FILTER, 2026-09-07 -- recorded as a\nSEAT FAILURE, never as agreement. ***  Zero-byte `.txt`, exit 0, the error only in the `.err`:\n*\"This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\"*  Full analysis and the two same-day\nnegative controls are on [[codexsubjectrefusal]].  **Re-fired on grok**, which reads the tree, takes\nits brief from a file and has no such filter -- so the question is covered, not lost.  The brief\nitself is preserved at `regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/WITNESS_brief.md` so the trigger can be\nstudied rather than guessed at.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN IS SOUND -- grok, 2026-09-07, ON THE BRIEF codex REFUSED.  The\nalternate-transport rule paid off completely: the recovery is BETTER than the refused seat would\nhave given, not a degraded substitute. ***\n\n**DENOMINATOR, and it is the largest this row has seen:** all of `dev_lca.c` (390 lines -- both\nhandlers, `DEVINIT`, ISA, interrupt stubs); `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` and\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)` end to end; `bus_pci_data_access`/`bus_pci_setaddr` in `bus_pci.c`;\n`alpha_translate_v2p` K0SEG in `memory_alpha.c`; the device and unmapped paths in `memory_rw.c`;\n`LS_GENERIC_N`/`LS_N` in `cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c`; `fatal()` in `debugmsg.c`; `ST_ALPHABOOK1`\nin `machine_alpha.c`; and all of `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`.\n\n**CONFIRMED:** S2 reaches `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` for ANY access in the window -- no `switch` on\n`relative_addr`, no `len` test, no `writeflag` test that can skip it, no early `return`; the only\n`writeflag` use before it is filling `idata`, which is not a guard.  `len` is first used AFTER the\nkill.  And `ioc_conf` has **exactly two readers**, so nothing else can be armed by S1.\n\n*** FOUR CORRECTIONS THAT CHANGE THE CONSTRUCTION: ***\n\n1. **MY LIVENESS CONTROL IS MISLABELLED.**  `ioc rt` proves the WRITER (`lca_ioc`'s CONF arm)\n   round-trips `0x1`.  **It says nothing about S2's window.**  PCI_CONF liveness is the armed\n   session's unique message (the handler ran) PLUS the control surviving (the same access is not an\n   unconditional kill).  A positive signature is available if wanted: an empty-slot read returns\n   `0xffffffffffffffff`, where unmapped returns 0.\n2. **S2 MUST BE A READ, NOT A WRITE.**  Both reach the kill, but an empty-slot WRITE makes\n   `bus_pci_data_access` print `[ ... write to non-existant device ... ]` -- so the CONTROL would\n   carry a second `fatal()` on stderr.  An empty-slot READ of reg 0 is the architectural ID probe\n   and is silent.\n3. **A TIGHTER VARIANT: ONE PROCESS, not two sessions.**  control `ldq` (survives), then S1, then\n   the armed `ldq` (dies) -- **only `ioc_conf` changes between the two loads.**  Separate sessions\n   are still adequate, but this removes the residual that two processes could differ in something\n   that ran before the debugger prompt.\n4. **KEEP IT ON `step`.**  `continue` would make the `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` stubs a\n   confounder -- and `cpu_dyntrans.c` skips interrupt delivery while single-stepping anyway.\n\n*** THE CONTRACT LIMIT, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE FOR THE FIX ROUND: THIS IS A WITNESS OF\nTHE `exit(1)`, NOT A DETECTOR OF THE REPAIR. ***  grok: *\"'Host survives, session completes' greens\nfor a silent `return 1` that skips `bus_pci_*` and `memory_writemax64`, for a latched announce, AND\nfor falling through into existing `bus_pci` with `cur_bus=1` (empty type-1 also survives).\"*  So the\npost-fix oracle cannot discriminate between fix members, and a round that ships one must build a row\nthat can.  **Post-fix caution in the same breath: if someone later implements type-1 by falling\nthrough to `bus_pci_setaddr(bus=1, ...)`, empty bus 1 is a non-killing miss today, so the witness\ngoes green -- do NOT read that green as \"type-1 is modelled.\"**\n\n**WHY OFFSET 0 IS THE RIGHT CONTROL ADDRESS, which I had asserted without warrant:** it is not\nHAE-like (HAE is an exact-address `break` in `lca_ioc`; `lca_pci_conf` has no per-offset special\ncase).  On the ARMED session the offset is irrelevant -- `bus == 1` exits before `tag`/`dev` are\nused.  On the SURVIVING control, `tag == 0` matches no IDSEL bit, so the `dev >= 21` body runs --\n**and that body is already the commented-out kill with `dev = 0;`** -- then an empty-slot read\nreturns `(uint64_t)-1` without exiting.  `0x18` (the NetBSD `| 0x3 << 3` encoding) yields the same\n`tag`/`reg` and is equivalent.  **Do NOT use `relative_addr = 0x800000`**, which talks to the\nregistered `i82378zb` at device 7.\n\n*** AND grok FOUND THE DESIGN ARGUMENT SITTING FIVE LINES ABOVE THE DEFECT: the `dev >= 21` arm is\nALREADY DE-FATALISED, in the same function, and the surviving control USES that path. ***  *\"If that\n`exit(1)` were still live, the control would die and you would misread PCI_CONF as unconditionally\nfatal.  The file already contains the decline precedent the `else` lacks.\"*  That is the strongest\navailable warrant for declining here, and it is one function away from the kill.\n\nAlso confirmed: FAULT (member 5) is unavailable -- `memory_rw.c` delivers a failed device access as\nan exception only under `MEM_MIPS`/`MEM_M88K`, and this path always `return 1` on the live path, so\nthe kill is `exit(1)` and not `return 0`.  And `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` kill is a DIFFERENT window\n(`LCA_PCI_SIO`), not reachable from `0x1e0000000`.\n\n*** CROSS-REFERENCE, AND A CORRECTION TO THIS ROW'S OWN NOVELTY CLAIM.  [[lcabadval]] ALREADY\nENUMERATED THIS KILL ON 2026-09-06. ***\n\nIts eleven-kill census for dev_lca.c lists **line 134, class BUS, condition `bus != 0`, i.e.\n`d->ioc_conf & 1`** -- so the site AND its dependence on ioc_conf were both already recorded, and I\nfiled this row saying only lcabadval mentioned lca_pci_conf without reading how.  **Sixth\nrediscovery this session; see [[refilingclass]], which predicts exactly this.**\n\n**WHAT THIS ROW GENUINELY ADDS, stated narrowly:** the census records the MECHANISM; it does not\nrecord that **`0x1` PASSES the CONF guard**.  That is what makes the kill reachable with no rejected\nvalue anywhere on the path -- an accepted write in one handler arming a terminating branch in\nanother -- and it is why the disposition differs from lcabadval's six value guards, whose kills all\nfire on refusal.  Plus the witness design, now reviewed SOUND by grok.\n\n**KEPT AS A SEPARATE ROW** because it has its own disposition (decline the config access, or\nimplement bus != 0) and its own witness, and because folding it into a row named for VALUE GUARDS\nwould hide it -- which is the census's own closing lesson: *a row named after one class hides the\nmembers of the others.*\n\n*** AND THE CENSUS NAMES SOMETHING PLAUSIBLY WORSE THAN THIS ROW, WHICH I SHOULD SAY OUT LOUD RATHER\nTHAN LEAVE IN A TABLE: the two INTERRUPT STUBS at :73 and :85. ***  `lca_interrupt_assert` and\n`lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)` with **NO GUARD AT ALL**, so *any*\ndevice asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it writes.  In the\ncensus's words: the value-guard registers *\"need a firmware revision that writes something else;\nthese two need only that an interrupt is asserted.\"*  That is member 7 (IMPLEMENT) of the option\nset -- a whole path stubbed out, with no value being refused -- and it is a stronger candidate for\nthe next critical round than this one.\n\n*** THE RUNG-3 WITNESS EXISTS AND THE DEFECT IS MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  regress/lca_confbus_probe.py,\nartifact regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/witness_HEAD_20260907.raw, sentinel LCACONFBUS_DONE. ***\n\n    bus ctl      yes                    yes                    CONTROL: identical read at ioc_conf==0 survived   ok\n    bus ctlsig   0xffffffffffffffff     0xffffffffffffffff     DEVICE SIGNATURE: empty-slot ID probe             ok\n    bus kill     dead                   alive                  <- THE DEFECT                                     FAIL\n    bus attrib   yes                    yes                    death carried THIS arm@s diagnostic               ok\n      EVIDENCE: TODO. Non-zero bus.\n    LCACONFBUS_RESULT=3/4\n\n**One emulator process, unmodified `alphabook1`, real guest instructions through real address\ndecode.**  The sequence is: guest `ldq` from `LCA_PCI_CONF+0` with `ioc_conf == 0` (SURVIVES), then\nguest `stq 0x1` to `LCA_IOC_CONF` (ACCEPTED -- `idata & ~1` is 0), then the IDENTICAL `ldq` again\n(HOST DIES).  Only `ioc_conf` differs between the two loads, which is what attributes the death to\nthe VALUE rather than to the address, the width, or touching the window at all.\n\n*** grok@s PREDICTED DEVICE SIGNATURE CAME BACK EXACTLY. ***  It reasoned from `bus_pci.c` that an\nempty-slot read returns `0xffffffffffffffff` via `memory_writemax64` while UNMAPPED returns 0.  The\ncontrol row reads **0xffffffffffffffff**.  That is a prediction from source, confirmed by\nmeasurement -- so the signature control is established, not assumed.\n\n**ORACLE POLARITY is the ladder@s: the rows assert the CORRECT (post-fix) behaviour, so `bus kill`\nis RED on the committed build and goes GREEN when the defect is fixed, with the SAME script.**\nClause (i) is therefore HALF discharged -- the pre-fix symptom is shown; the post-fix green awaits a\nfix, which is correct for test-first.\n\n*** TWO APPARATUS DEFECTS CAUGHT IN THE FIRST RUN AND FIXED, both worth keeping. ***\n1. **The first artifact carried NO EVIDENCE for `bus attrib`.**  The row tested the PTY buffer and\n   printed nothing, so the preserved log showed a green row whose diagnostic a later reader could\n   not check.  The probe now ECHOES the matched line into its own output.  *A green row whose\n   evidence is not in the artifact is not verifiable, however sound the logic.*\n2. **I nearly filed a false correction to grok.**  It said the `TODO. Non-zero bus.` literal is\n   unique in `src/`; my grep returned TWO.  The second hit is `dev_lca.o`, the compiled object.  One\n   source site, `dev_lca.c:133`.  grok was right; my apparatus swept binaries.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS CLEARER THAN THIS ROW ASSUMED, AND IT DIVERGES FROM ITS SIBLING FOR A REASON\nWORTH STATING.  Derived 2026-09-07 from the codex fix design on [[lcairqstubkill]] plus this row's\nown witness. ***\n\n**THE TWO LCA ROWS LOOK LIKE ONE PROBLEM AND ARE NOT.**  Both are bare `fatal()+exit(1)` reached by\nordinary guest access, so it is tempting to give them one disposition.  The difference is what the\nguest is ASKING FOR:\n\n* [[lcairqstubkill]] -- an interrupt DELIVERY.  There is no correct answer that is not delivery: a\n  decline LOSES AN EVENT, silently or otherwise, and the guest can never learn of it.  codex's\n  disposition there is \"diagnose and stop the guest CPU\", because continuing is a lie.\n* **THIS ROW -- a PCI configuration cycle on bus 1.  There IS a correct answer, and hardware gives\n  it: a config read of an absent device returns ALL ONES.**  An unimplemented BUS is legitimately\n  EMPTY.  Declining is not a lost event; it is the architecturally right reply.\n\n> **So the dispositions properly differ, and the reason is not \"one is worse\".  For a DELIVERY path\n> an unimplemented target has no honest continuation; for an ADDRESSING path it has one, and the\n> tree already implements it.**\n\n*** AND THE TREE ALREADY DOES THE RIGHT THING ONE STEP DOWN, which the witness measured by accident.\n***  The `bus == 0` branch already reaches `bus_pci_setaddr` / `bus_pci_data_access`, and this row's\nown witness recorded the result: **a read of an empty slot returns `0xffffffffffffffff`** (the\n`bus ctlsig` row, MEASURED).  grok noted the same about the armed side in its design review --\n*\"falling through into existing bus_pci with cur_bus=1 (empty type-1 also survives)\"*.\n\n**SO THE LIKELY FIX IS NOT A DECLINE AT ALL BUT A FALL-THROUGH:** stop special-casing `bus != 0`,\nlet the existing `bus_pci` path handle it, and an empty bus 1 answers all-ones by itself.  That is\noption-set member 7 (IMPLEMENT) reached cheaply, because the machinery exists -- unlike the sibling\nrow, where member 7 is blocked at the dyntrans core.\n\n*** THE DISCRIMINATING ROW IS THEREFORE THE RETURNED VALUE, NOT THE RUNNING STATE. ***  codex's\noracle for the sibling row (\"stopped guest state, plus the reason\") is right THERE and wrong HERE,\nbecause a correct fix must leave the guest RUNNING.  The observable that separates the candidates\nhere is what the armed read returns:\n\n    fall through to bus_pci (empty bus 1)      0xffffffffffffffff    <- architecturally correct\n    silent return without servicing            0x0 or unchanged      <- looks like UNMAPPED\n    latched announce then return unserviced    0x0 or unchanged      <- same, plus a message\n    the existing exit(1)                       host gone\n\n**And this probe ALREADY HAS THAT ROW SHAPE**: `bus ctlsig` asserts exactly `0xffffffffffffffff` on\nthe bus-0 control, and distinguishes it from the `0x0` an unmapped read gives.  The fix round adds\nthe mirror of it on the ARMED side.  *No new capability is needed, which is not true of the sibling.*\n\n**NOT YET VERIFIED, and it is the one thing to check before building:** that `bus_pci` genuinely\ntolerates `cur_bus == 1` rather than asserting or indexing something sized for one bus.  grok's\nreview says empty type-1 survives; **that is a seat's reading and this row has not measured it.**\nThe cheap check is to build the fall-through and run the existing probe -- the armed row should turn\nfrom `dead` to `0xffffffffffffffff`.\n\n*** THE UNVERIFIED LINK IS CLOSED BY READING: `bus_pci' IS BUS-AGNOSTIC BY CONSTRUCTION, SO THE\nFALL-THROUGH FIX IS SOUND WITHOUT A NEW CAPABILITY. ***\n\nThe paragraph above flagged one thing to check before building -- whether `bus_pci' tolerates\n`cur_bus == 1' rather than asserting or indexing something sized for a single bus.  It does:\n\n    bus_pci.h:84   int  cur_bus, cur_device, cur_func, cur_reg;   <- a plain int, not bus-0-only\n    bus_pci.h:87   struct pci_device *first_device;               <- a LINKED LIST, not an array\n                                                                     indexed by bus number\n    bus_pci.c:102  if (dev->bus == pci_data->cur_bus && ...)      <- devices carry their OWN bus\n                                                                     field and are MATCHED against it\n    bus_pci.c:200  pci_data->cur_bus = bus;                       <- set from the caller, unchecked\n\n**So a lookup with `cur_bus = 1` walks the list, matches nothing (no device carries `bus == 1`), and\nfalls into the no-device path -- which this row's own witness has already MEASURED returning\n`0xffffffffffffffff` on bus 0.**  Nothing is sized for one bus and nothing asserts.\n\n*** THAT CONFIRMS grok's READING AND UPGRADES IT FROM A SEAT CLAIM TO A CHECKED ONE. ***  Its design\nreview said *\"falling through into existing bus_pci with cur_bus=1 (empty type-1 also survives)\"*,\nwhich this row correctly recorded as unmeasured.  It is now verified by source rather than accepted.\n\n**CONSEQUENCE: the fix is a DELETION, not an addition.**  Remove the `bus != 0` special case and let\nthe existing path serve the cycle; an absent device on an absent bus answers all-ones by itself.\nThat is the cheapest member-7 in the option set -- and the addition licence is satisfied trivially,\nbecause nothing is added.\n\n**WHAT STILL OWES A MEASUREMENT, and it is one run:** build the fall-through and re-run\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`.  **PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION: the `bus kill' row turns from `dead'\nto `alive', and a mirror of `bus ctlsig' on the armed side should read `0xffffffffffffffff`** -- the\nsame value the bus-0 control already returns, which is what distinguishes a serviced empty-bus cycle\nfrom a silent unserviced return (`0x0`, indistinguishable from unmapped).\n\n*** PASS-1 FIX PANEL, 2026-09-07 -- PROVISIONAL: recorded on THREE landed seats (deepseek, minimax,\nthe kimi-k3:cloud substitute) while codex, grok and glm were still answering.  Any ruling short of\nthe roster is provisional on its face. ***\n\n**Q1 IS SETTLED AND MY WORRY WAS UNFOUNDED -- AND THE PROOF IS INSIDE THE EXISTING CODE, WHICH I HAD\nNOT THOUGHT TO USE.**  I asked whether the file's tag comment describes the same `tag` this code\ncomputes (`(relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff`) or a pre-shift value.  minimax:\n\n> *\"The existing IDSEL check proves the code's `tag` IS the comment's `tag`.  The `bus == 0` branch\n> checks `tag & (0x800U << dev)` for dev 0..20.  The mask `1 << (dev+11)` only matches real hardware\n> if `tag` holds IDSEL bits at positions 11..31 -- exactly the comment's type-0 layout.  That branch\n> is EXERCISED TODAY on real bus-0 traffic and works, so the interpretation is FORCED.\"*\n\n`0x800` is exactly `1 << 11`, and the kimi substitute reaches it independently (*\"func at bits 10..8\nin both rows\"*).  **So `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is the faithful type-1 decode.**  *A working branch\nof the same function is a stronger oracle for a layout question than the comment is -- the comment\ncould be stale, the working code cannot be.*\n\n*** Q2 IS THE REAL DEFECT IN MY SKETCH, AND ALL THREE SEATS FLAG IT: THE FIX AS WRITTEN WOULD BE\nINCOHERENT. ***  deepseek: *\"the sketch is incomplete/incoherent because it keeps\n`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` while the type-1 tag comment says bus is in tag bits 23..16.\"*  The kimi\nsubstitute sharpens it: **the model computes THREE bus/type sources from the address -- the AD[1:0]\ntype bits, the tag's bus field, and `ioc_conf & 1` -- and DISCARDS TWO.**  *\"The packet cannot say\nwhich one real hardware honours.\"*\n\n> **So decoding `dev` from the type-1 layout while taking `bus` from a register bit mixes two\n> addressing models.**  Either the tag is authoritative (and `bus` must come from bits 23..16) or the\n> register is (and the type-1 tag layout does not apply).  **The fix cannot be written until that is\n> chosen, and no seat could choose it from the packet.**\n\n**Q4: THE DISCRIMINATING ROW IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT -- unanimous among the three.**  My\nproposed armed-side all-ones row separates *serviced an empty bus* from *returned without servicing*\n(`0x0`, indistinguishable from unmapped).  **It cannot separate IMPLEMENT from an explicit all-ones\nreturn**, because an EMPTY bus 1 answers all-ones under both.  kimi: *\"only a populated bus-1 device\ncan\"* tell them apart -- and nothing in this tree places one.\n\n*That is a real limit on what this round can claim, and it is better to know it before building the\nrow than after.*\n\n**NOT ADOPTED PENDING THE ROSTER.**  The Q2 question is a hardware-behaviour question the packet\ncannot settle, and `_scratchpad/` holds no LCA/21066 datasheet (the `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` list has\nDC21285 but no 21066), so it may not be settleable from this machine at all -- which would make it a\ncandidate for the 2026-09-09 adjudication rather than for a fix round.\n\n*** grok LANDED AND SETTLED Q2 -- THE ANSWER THE PACKET-FED SEATS COULD NOT REACH, AND IT DISSOLVES\nTHE INCOHERENCE RATHER THAN CHOOSING A SIDE. ***\n\n> **VERDICT: IMPLEMENT, but the sketch is one assignment short.**  `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is the\n> right extract of *this* `tag` (Q1 is not a double-shift).  **Also assign\n> `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff` -- `ioc_conf & 1` is the PCI cycle TYPE, not the bus number.**\n> Member 7; fall through into `bus_pci`.  The proposed all-ones row is necessary and not sufficient:\n> the witness address is not a type-1 tag, and empty-slot data cannot pin the extract.\n\n**WHY THIS RESOLVES THE CONFLICT INSTEAD OF PICKING A WINNER.**  deepseek and the kimi substitute\nwere right that taking `dev` from the tag while taking `bus` from a register bit mixes two addressing\nmodels.  grok's reading shows the register bit is not a bus selector at all: it chooses the CYCLE\nTYPE -- type 0 addresses the local bus, type 1 addresses a bus behind a bridge -- and in type 1 the\nbus number comes from the tag.  **The file's own comment, \"the PCI bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit\",\nis a loose description of exactly that**, so the code comment and grok agree once the names are\nstraightened.  *The variable is simply misnamed: `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;` stores a cycle type in\nsomething called `bus`.*\n\n**THE FIX IS THEREFORE TWO ASSIGNMENTS, NOT ONE:**\n\n    } else {\n            bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;    /*  type-1: bus is bits 23..16  */\n            dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;    /*  type-1: device is bits 15..11  */\n    }\n\n*** AND grok BREAKS MY PROPOSED DISCRIMINATING ROW FOR A REASON I HAD NOT SEEN: \"the witness address\nis not a type-1 tag\". ***  The existing probe reads at `LCA_PCI_CONF + 0`, so `tag == 0` -- which\nunder the type-1 decode means bus 0, device 0.  **The row would therefore exercise the new code with\na degenerate tag and prove nothing about the extract**, and \"empty-slot data cannot pin the extract\"\nbecause an absent device answers all-ones whatever bus/dev were decoded.\n\n> **So the round needs a row that drives a NON-ZERO type-1 tag and observes something that depends\n> on the decoded bus/dev -- and with no device on bus 1, there is nothing to observe.**  That is the\n> same limit the three packet-fed seats reached from the other side (*\"only a populated bus-1 device\n> can\"* separate IMPLEMENT from a faked all-ones return).  Two independent routes to one conclusion.\n\n**HARDWARE CLAIM, FLAGGED AS UNVERIFIABLE FROM THIS MACHINE:** that `ioc_conf & 1` is the cycle type\nis a statement about the 21066/LCA, and **`_scratchpad/` holds no LCA datasheet** -- the PDF list has\nDC21285 but nothing for this chipset.  It is consistent with the file's comment and with standard PCI\ntype-0/type-1 addressing, but it is not CHECKED here, and this project's rule is to cite only what\nhas been read.  **That is a good candidate for the 2026-09-09 adjudication rather than for me.**\n\n*** THE PANEL IS COMPLETE (6 seats) AND UNANIMOUS ON THE DESIGN -- AND codex CITED A PRIMARY SOURCE\nSITTING ON THIS MACHINE THAT I HAD JUST DECLARED UNAVAILABLE. ***\n\nI wrote, one paragraph above, that the cycle-type claim is *\"a statement about the 21066 ... not\nCHECKED here\"* because `_scratchpad/` holds no LCA datasheet.  **codex pointed at\n`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c` -- the REAL OpenBSD LCA driver, 4,925 bytes,\ndated 1997, on disk in this project.**  Verified and read:\n\n    :103   if (bp != NULL)  *bp = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;      /*  BUS      */\n    :106   if (dp != NULL)  *dp = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;      /*  DEVICE   */\n    :108   if (fp != NULL)  *fp = (tag >>  8) & 0x7;       /*  FUNCTION */\n\n**That is grok's proposal, verbatim, from the driver the hardware was written for.**  `bus` is bits\n23..16 of the tag and `dev` is bits 15..11 -- not inference, not a datasheet paraphrase, the actual\ndecomposition routine.\n\n*** AND `lca_conf_read` SETTLES Q2 OUTRIGHT: ***\n\n    :125   /* secondary if bus # != 0 */\n    :126   pci_decompose_tag(&lcp->lc_pc, tag, &secondary, &device, 0);\n    :127   if (secondary) {\n    :130           REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01;\n    :132   } else {\n    :134           /*  on the LCA, must frob the tag used for devices on the primary bus,\n    :136              in the same ways as is used by type 1 configuration cycles on PCs.  */\n    :139           tag = (1 << (device + 11)) | (tag & 0x7ff);\n    :140   }\n\n**The driver derives the bus from the TAG and then writes `IOC_CONF = 0x01` to select the SECONDARY\nconfiguration FORMAT.**  So `ioc_conf & 1` is the cycle type, exactly as grok and codex said and as\nthe fork's own loosely-worded comment (\"bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit\") was groping at.  **The\nfork's variable is misnamed**, and its type-0 handling is correct -- `(1 << (device + 11))` is\nprecisely the IDSEL form the `0x800U << dev` loop scans for.\n\n*** THE ROW IS MASSIVELY STRENGTHENED, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. ***  My witness writes\n`0x1` to `LCA_IOC_CONF` and then performs a config access, and I described that as an ordinary but\nsynthetic pair of guest accesses.  **It is not synthetic: it is the OpenBSD driver's own\nsecondary-bus path, line for line.**  `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` followed by the config read is\nwhat `lca_conf_read` DOES whenever it is asked about a device behind a bridge.\n\n> **So the kill is not merely reachable in principle -- it is on the code path a real guest driver\n> takes the moment it probes a secondary bus.**  The row previously said \"NOT CLAIMED: that any\n> shipped guest issues this sequence.\"  That caution can now be withdrawn: a shipped guest driver\n> issues exactly this sequence, by construction, for secondary-bus config cycles.\n\n**THE FIX, now backed by the driver rather than by reading the fork's comment:**\n\n    } else {\n            bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;    /*  type-1: bus is bits 23..16   */\n            dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;    /*  type-1: device is bits 15..11 */\n    }\n\n**WHAT IS STILL NOT SETTLED, and every seat agrees:** the discriminating row.  grok -- *\"the witness\naddress is not a type-1 tag, and empty-slot data cannot pin the extract\"*; glm -- the row *\"conflates\nIMPLEMENT with explicit-all-ones DECLINE, silent with latched, and can false-pass on stale all-ones\nunless the destination register is pre-seeded\"*; the kimi substitute and minimax -- only a POPULATED\nbus-1 device could separate them.  **Four independent routes to one wall.**  glm's pre-seeding point\nis the one actionable improvement: seed the destination register so a stale all-ones cannot pass.\n\n*The lesson about this machine is the `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` lesson again, one directory over: I\ndeclared a fact unverifiable while its primary source sat in the project.  **Before calling a\nhardware question unanswerable here, look for the guest OS source tree.***\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** codex: REVISE.  THE SHIFT IS RIGHT AND ALMOST EVERYTHING AROUND IT NEEDED CORRECTING --\nINCLUDING TWO PREMISES I STATED AS SETTLED. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, with a denominator.**  `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is correct: `relative_addr >> 5`\nundoes the address expansion, so for a valid tag T and aligned register R,\n`A = (T << 5) | (R << 5) | 24` gives `((A >> 5) & ~0xff) == T`, putting device bits at 15..11 of\nthe recovered tag.  Validated over **4,194,304 encode/decode combinations, zero mismatches** --\nand codex labelled that an ARITHMETIC check, not an emulator test, which is the honest form.\n\n*** MY IMPLEMENT SKETCH WAS INCOMPLETE: IT DECODED `dev` AND NOT `bus`. ***  `IOC_CONF` selects\nthe CYCLE FORMAT, it does not supply the bus number -- PCI address bits 23..2 come from CPU\naddress bits 28..7, and only bits 1..0 come from `IOC_CONF[1:0]`.  So the minimum correction is\n\n    } else {\n        bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;\n        dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;\n    }\n\n> **AND THE TRAP IN THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE, which I would have walked into: DO NOT CHOOSE THE\n> DECODING BRANCH FROM THE EXTRACTED BUS FIELD.**  Primary-bus IDSEL bits overlap that field, and\n> keeping `bus == 1` in the forwarding call **collapses every type-1 destination onto bus 1**.\n\n*** PREMISE 4 IS FALSE AS STATED, and the refutation is one function away. ***  I wrote that an\nempty-slot read returns all-ones.  `bus_pci_data_access` implements a THREE-WAY contract:\n\n    read, register 0        ->  0xffffffffffffffff\n    read, any other reg     ->  ZERO\n    write                   ->  a diagnostic on EVERY access, then returns\n\nThe measured ID-register instance stands; the GENERAL claim does not.  **This matters because it\nmeans the candidate dispositions differ TODAY, with no bus-1 device added at all** -- an\nunconditional all-ones responder differs from the backend at nonzero registers, and announcement\npolicies differ on diagnostic output.  (The write diagnostic does not terminate: `fatal()` returns\nnormally.)\n\n*** THE DISCRIMINATING ROW I PROPOSED CANNOT DISCRIMINATE, AND IT HAS THREE FALSE-POSITIVE TRAPS.\n***  At register 0, all-ones is returned by correct forwarding, by the INCORRECT bus-fixed-to-1\nforwarding, by explicit all-ones completion, and by announced all-ones completion -- four\ndispositions, one observable.  Worse:\n\n* **THE EXISTING ADDRESS DOES NOT ENCODE BUS 1.**  `lca_confbus_probe.py`'s setup uses a ZERO\n  window offset, so the recovered tag -- bus field included -- is zero, and the control side\n  reaches the **no-IDSEL fallback to device 0**.  *The witness establishes mode-dependent\n  termination, not a correctly addressed secondary-bus transaction.*  That is a scope correction\n  to what this row's existing witness is entitled to claim.\n* **A FAILED LOAD PRESERVES THE CONTROL SIGNATURE.**  `memory_rw` pre-zeros, so a handler that\n  returns success without filling data yields zero -- but a literal `return 0` takes the FAILURE\n  path, and `LS_GENERIC_N` then returns *before updating the destination register*, so the witness\n  **reads back the all-ones the register already held.**  A vacuity trap of exactly the shape the\n  carrier's witness clauses exist to catch.\n* **THE PARSER TAKES THE FIRST OBSERVATION** (`printed` uses `re.search`), so adding another print\n  and calling the helper again retrieves the CONTROL's result rather than the new one.\n\n**WHAT IT PRESCRIBES INSTEAD:** keep the signature check as an empty-ID COMPLETION check with an\nindependently identified result and a distinct prior destination value, and add a **model-level\nrouting fixture** observing the exact bus/device/function/register passed onward -- distinct\nsynthetic devices on bus 1 and on another bus separate correct decoding from hardcoded bus 1 and\nfrom constant-return stubs.  Include write dispatch and preservation of primary-bus decoding.\n\n*** A SECOND ARCHITECTURAL PREMISE CORRECTED: RAW LCA ABSENCE DOES NOT GUARANTEE ALL-ONES. ***\nDigital's §6.2.18 says an unanswered transaction is a MASTER ABORT: the read returns *arbitrary\ndata with error status*, raising an instruction exception plus interrupt and IOC error logging.\n**So the 64-bit all-ones signature is an EMULATOR-COMPATIBILITY oracle, not a hardware contract**,\nand the round may not claim hardware fidelity for it.  The current path delegates to the generic\nbackend and returns success; it implements none of that error sequence.\n\n**SCOPE, stated so the round does not overclaim what it closes:** this closes the configuration\nhandler's termination and its missing destination decode ONLY.  Still open in the same file --\n`lca_ioc`'s unconditional read/write `default` termination and its register-value rejections;\n`lca_interrupt_assert` / `lca_interrupt_deassert`; and `lca_isa`'s separate guard.  Nor does\nforwarding implement bridge topology or configuration-cycle error reporting: the backend matches a\nnumeric tuple and receives **no cycle-type argument at all**, so type 1 with a zero bus field needs\nan explicit policy or simple forwarding will alias a root-bus device.\n\n**PRIMARY SOURCE, and this project did not have it:** codex cited the *Digital 21066/21066A\nHardware Reference Manual* (Table 6-1, §6.4.2, §6.2.18) with page anchors.  No 21066/LCA manual was\nin `_scratchpad/`, and there are several open LCA rows reasoning from source alone.  Fetched at the\ncitation, on the `vmesbcp2.pdf` precedent.\n\n*** AND THEN VERIFIED LOCALLY RATHER THAN TRUSTED, which is what the carrier's rule demands and\nwhich this project has been burned by skipping: `#357`/`#362`/`#365` shipped DDI 0100I page numbers\nthat came from panel output rather than a manual. ***  `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt`, 713 KB /\n13,876 lines:\n\n    :8840  S6.2.6   \"CPU physical address bits <31:7> are used for PCI address bits <26:2>\"\n                    -> THE SHIFT OF 5 that `relative_addr >> 5` implements.  CONFIRMED.\n    :9216  S6.2.9   \"PCI address bits ad<1:0> come directly from configuration cycle type\n                     register (IOC_CONF, Section 6.4.2) bits <1:0>\"\n                    -> IOC_CONF selects the CYCLE TYPE and does NOT supply the bus.  CONFIRMED.\n    :9486  S6.2.18  \"the IOC returns arbitrary data with error status to the CPU, which causes\n                     an instruction exception\" + internal interrupt + ERR/CODE=1/CMD/ADDR/LOST\n                    -> absent-device reads are NOT all-ones on real hardware.  CONFIRMED.\n\n**One narrowing, stated because a wrong reason gets reused:** codex wrote *\"bits 23..2 from CPU\naddress bits 28..7\"*.  The manual's general sparse rule is the WIDER `<31:7> -> <26:2>` with the\nsame shift; codex's range is the PCI **type-1 config format** (bus 23..16, dev 15..11, fn 10..8,\nreg 7..2), which S6.2.9 defers to explicitly -- *\"restricted by the encoding requirements of PCI\nconfiguration cycles as described in the PCI Local Bus [Specification]\"*.  **Same shift, narrower\nwindow, and the narrowing is correct for config cycles** -- so `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff` and\n`dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` rest on the PCI spec's field layout, not on this manual, and the round\nshould say so rather than citing the LCA manual for the field positions.\n\n*** A TRAP FOR THE NEXT READER: `file` REPORTS THIS PDF AS \"10 page(s)\" AND IT IS WRONG. ***  The\nreal count lives in a linearized trailer.  I nearly discarded the download as truncated because\ncodex had cited `#page=194` and `#page=211`; `pdftotext -layout` settled it in one command.  *A\nsize or page check that disagrees with a citation is a reason to extract, not to discard.*\n\n*** I RE-DERIVED THIS ROW'S GUEST-SOURCE EVIDENCE FROM SCRATCH AND EVERY ELEMENT OF IT WAS ALREADY\nHERE.  Caught before committing, by grepping this note instead of trusting my memory of it. ***\n\nI read `_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c` and wrote up four findings as new: that\n`lca_conf_read` writes `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` on the secondary-bus path; that `data` is\ninitialised to `(pcireg_t)-1` and overwritten only if `badaddr()` succeeds, so absence normalises to\nall-ones at ANY offset; that the primary-bus tag frob places an IDSEL bit at `1 << (device + 11)`;\nand the decomposition itself.  **Counted in this very note before adding them: `REGVAL` 4, `IDSEL`\n7, `device + 11` 4, `badaddr` 2, `pcireg_t)-1` 1.**  All four were already recorded, several in the\nsame words.\n\n**THE CAUSE IS EXACT AND IT IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY: I READ PART OF A RECORD AND ACTED.**  I\nqueried this row's `state_note` and looked at the first fifteen hundred characters, which covered\nthe defect and the shipped witness but stopped short of the guest-source section.  Earlier the same\nday I read a row's prose and never read its `witness` FIELD, and built a second witness for a row\nthat already had one.  *Different field, same failure: a partial read of a record that already\ncontained the answer.*\n\n**WHAT IS ACTUALLY NEW FROM MY PASS is one caveat, and it belongs to the reachability claim rather\nthan to the decode:** the driver's `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` sits behind `if (secondary)`, so it\nexecutes only when something addresses a bus other than zero -- and **whether `alphabook1` ever\npresents a secondary bus is not established anywhere in this row.**  `devinit_lca` adds only\n`i82378zb` at 0:7:0.  So *the driver contains the sequence unconditionally; whether a booting guest\nexecutes it is a topology fact nobody here has measured.*  That bounds how far the guest source can\ncarry the reachability argument, and it is the one line worth keeping from an otherwise redundant\nre-derivation.\n\n*The `guestsrcunknown` row deliberately FILED its proposed carrier line for the 2026-09-09\nadjudication rather than editing the carrier unilaterally.  I have honoured that: today's two\ncarrier edits were INVENTORY entries in an existing PDF list, not a new behavioural rule.*\n\n*** THE REVISED DESIGN IS A DIFF, NOT A SHAPE -- AND IT REVERSES ITS OWN EARLIER SCOPE POSITION. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Include the bus-zero alias in this round.  A single-handler diff can fix it without\nchanging the backend API.  My earlier proposal was incomplete; the replacement observer remains\nunvalidated here.\"*\n\n**THE KEY CORRECTION IS ONE VARIABLE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE DEFECT IN MINIATURE:** the handler used a\nsingle `bus` for two different things -- the CYCLE TYPE selected by `ioc_conf & 1`, and the\nDESTINATION bus.  The diff splits them:\n\n    -\tint tag, bus, dev, func, reg;\n    +\tint tag, type1, bus, dev, func, reg;\n    ...\n    -\tbus = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    -\tif (bus == 0) {\n    +\ttype1 = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    +\tbus = 0;\n    +\tif (!type1) {\n\nthen decodes the real destination on the type-1 arm -- `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff; dev = (tag >> 11)\n& 0x1f;` -- replacing the `fatal(); exit(1)`.\n\n*** AND IT CLOSES THE ALIAS IT ITSELF FOUND, WHICH THE EARLIER \"MINIMUM CORRECTION\" DID NOT: ***\n\n    +\tif (type1 && bus == 0) {\n    +\t\t/*  Type-1 cycles do not select primary-bus devices.\n    +\t\t    Match bus_pci's empty-slot reads; discard writes.  */\n    +\t\todata = reg == 0 ? (uint64_t) -1 : 0;\n    +\t} else {\n    \t\tbus_pci_setaddr(...); bus_pci_data_access(...);\n    +\t}\n\n**The refusal covers reads AND writes and leaves backend state untouched**, and its return values\nmatch `bus_pci_data_access`'s own empty-slot contract -- all-ones at register 0, zero elsewhere --\nwhich is the contract I stated WRONGLY as a premise two passes ago and which codex corrected.\n\n> **SO THE ROUND'S SCOPE QUESTION IS ANSWERED AGAINST MY NARROWER FRAMING.**  I asked it to argue\n> the narrower round first; it did, and then held that the alias must land in the same diff -- on\n> the ground that forwarding a type-1 cycle with bus zero would select the existing primary-bus\n> `i82378zb`, so a round that decodes `bus` and stops there ships a NEW misrouting in place of a\n> termination.\n\n**HONEST LIMITS, stated by the seat rather than extracted from it:** the diff is **unapplied and\nuncompiled** -- it passes `git apply --check` and nothing more, because that session is read-only --\nand **the replacement observer remains unvalidated**, so the round still has no measurement lane\nbeyond the existing 3/4 witness.  *A design that says which of its parts are untested is worth more\nthan one that does not, and this round must not present the diff as measured.*\n\n*** #515 IMPLEMENTED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-08.  6/10 PRE-FIX -> 10/10 POST-FIX, and the round found\na FLOOR BREACH IN ITS OWN DETECTOR before any table ran. ***\n\n**THE DIFF** is codex's revised design applied unchanged in substance: `bus' split into `type1'\n(the CYCLE TYPE, `d->ioc_conf & 1') and `bus' (the DESTINATION, decoded from the address), the\n`fatal(\"TODO. Non-zero bus.\"); exit(1)' arm replaced by `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff; dev = (tag >> 11)\n& 0x1f;', and a refusal arm for the type-1/bus-zero alias returning `bus_pci_data_access''s own\nempty-slot values.  40 insertions, 9 deletions, 47 changed lines.\n\n**THE FUNCTION TAIL IS PRESERVED ON BOTH ARMS** -- `memory_writemax64` and `return 1` are still\nreached, so this is not the early-`return' sketch four readers once proposed into a different\nfunction.  Checked by reading the whole function after the edit, not by reading the diff.\n\n*** MEASUREMENT, in a scratch clone with the baseline NAMED and REBUILT. ***  `/tmp/gxlca`, a\nrelocatable `cp -a` of `build/`; baseline = git HEAD `5d3f82c`; `dev_lca.c` `cmp`-clean against SEC\nbefore mutant 1; baseline binary rebuilt IN THE CLONE with `[ binary -nt source ]` checked.\n`build/`, `est/`, `build-asan/` and the WSL tree were NOT touched.\n\n    PRE-FIX   LCACONFBUS_RESULT=6/10    pass2_20260908/lca_pre.raw\n    POST-FIX  LCACONFBUS_RESULT=10/10   pass2_20260908/lca_post.raw\n\nBoth verified by their completion sentinel (`LCACONFBUS_DONE`), not by existence.\n\n**THE ANCHOR ROW PRINTED THE DERIVED TUPLE EXACTLY**, which is the whole point of an anchored row:\n\n    EVIDENCE(S2): [ bus_pci_data_access(): write to non-existant device, bus 1 func 2 device 3 ]\n\nAddresses were derived from the handler's own two decode lines and then CONFIRMED by that line --\n`bus 1 dev 3 func 2` from `relative_addr = 0x234000`.  The three fields are deliberately DISTINCT\nand non-zero so a swapped field cannot pass by coincidence.\n\n*** TWO DEFECTS IN MY OWN DETECTOR, BOTH FOUND BEFORE COMMIT, AND THE SECOND IS A FLOOR BREACH. ***\n\n**(1) A VACUOUS ROW, caught by the PRE-FIX RUN rather than by review.**  `t1 alias' first read\n`refused if ALIAS not in txt2' and scored GREEN on the pre-fix build -- where the host had already\ndied at the previous access and the alias write NEVER EXECUTED.  An absence-of-text oracle cannot\ndistinguish *\"ran and was refused\"* from *\"never ran\"*: the pattern-that-matched-nothing class, as a\nphantom absence read as a result.  Bound to `alive2' and now renders `died-before-E'.  *The row\nwould have shipped green-for-the-wrong-reason on the very build it exists to indict.*\n\n**(2) A CLAUSE-(d) NULL-REPAIR MUTANT THAT SWEEPS A 10-ROW DETECTOR CLEAN, found by walking the\nCHANNEL line at row-writing time.**  The refusal's repair is one expression -- `odata = reg == 0?\n(uint64_t) -1 : 0'.  Null it to `odata = 0' and every one of the ten rows still passes: the alias\nwas exercised only by a WRITE, whose `odata' is never read, and both control reads take the type-0\npath.  ROW `t1 rdval' added -- a type-1 READ at the alias, pinning the synthesised value.\n\n> *** MY OWN PASS-2 BRIEF ASSERTED THAT CELL WAS UNOBSERVABLE, AND IT IS NOT. ***  I wrote that\n> (type-1, bus == 0, read) *\"returns the same all-ones value as the control, so a read oracle cannot\n> see it\"*.  That conflates two different accesses: the control is a TYPE-0 empty-slot read served\n> by `bus_pci`, and this is the REFUSAL arm's own synthesised value.  They agree under the fix and\n> DISAGREE under the mutant, which is exactly what makes a row possible.  The brief had already gone\n> to the panel when I found it; it goes to the adjudication pass as my own correction.\n\n*** THE PRE-TABLE CHECK WAS RUN AND ITS OUTPUT RECORDED BEFORE TABLE 1, mtime 21:35:00 against a\nbrief of 21:32:10 -- the order is the proof, and it is the artifact rather than an assertion. ***\n`pass2_20260908/PRETABLE.out`, all four checks with `N/A` discharged WITH REASONS on the three\ncontrol rows rather than left blank.\n\n> **AND THE CARRIER ASKED THE FIRST MANDATED APPLICATION TO *CHECK* THE CHANNEL LINE, NOT MERELY\n> OBEY IT -- because a freshly minted rule's exemplar is a hypothesis.  THE ANSWER IS THAT ITS CLAIM\n> HELD.**  The claim is that a row's channel-under-its-mutant is knowable at WRITING time, before\n> any table runs.  It was, and doing it found finding (2) above -- a floor breach -- with no build\n> and no measurement.  Recorded as a measurement OF the rule, not as praise for it.\n\n*** THE ASK-AGAIN: ONE CAPPED QUERY, THREE REAL ESCAPES, ALL THE SAME FAMILY.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nTable 2 came back CLEAN -- ten variants, zero escapes -- so the round spent its single capped\nadversarial query.  **The cap counts ASKS, never FINDINGS**, so all three are disposed of here rather\nthan one taken and two filed.\n\n    deepseek   `if (type1 && bus == 0 && len == 8)'\n               Every row used an 8-byte access, so all fourteen stay GREEN, while a 1/2/4-byte\n               type-1 bus-0 access bypasses the refusal and reaches `bus_pci_data_access' --\n               including the REAL device at 0:7:0.\n    kimi       `func = (tag >> 8) & 7'  ->  `& 3'\n               The table's only func probe was the anchor's func 2 (binary 010), which never\n               exercises the field's TOP bit.  Functions 4-7 silently alias onto 0-3, so a config\n               cycle aimed at dev 7 FUNC 4 matches the registered func-0 device.\n    codex      the `reg != 0' branch of the refusal's own expression\n               *** RANKED ABOVE THE OTHER TWO BY THE SEAT ITSELF, and it is right. ***  `len' and\n               `func' are values no row varies; `reg != 0' is a WHOLE BRANCH of `odata = reg == 0?\n               (uint64_t) -1 : 0' that nothing reached.  Killing `M2_nullval' proves sensitivity to\n               the register-ZERO result and establishes nothing about the required zero elsewhere.\n\n> **ONE FAMILY: AN AXIS EVERY EXISTING ROW HELD CONSTANT.**  len was 8 everywhere, func's top bit was\n> never set, reg was 0 everywhere.  Fourteen rows, three frozen axes -- and the rows were mutually\n> reinforcing on the axes they DID vary, which is exactly what makes a frozen axis invisible.\n\n*** AND THE THIRD ONE WAS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN IN THIS ROUND'S OWN PRE-TABLE BLOCK AND WALKED PAST. ***\nSection 2 of `PRETABLE.out` says, in its own words: *\"THE `zero elsewhere' HALF IS NOT ROWED ...\nDISCLOSED, not silently skipped ... Coverage debt, clause (e).\"*  A disclosure is an honest record and\nit is not a row; **the seat found in one query what the round had already identified and deferred.**\nThat is the unread-review shape aimed at a round's own artifact rather than at a seat's answer.\n\n**FOUR ROWS ADDED, 14 -> 18** (`t1 len4`, `t0 func4`, `t1 reg0`, `t1 reg4`).  All L1 construction --\ncaught before the detector shipped.\n\n**DISPOSITION, stated rather than assumed.**  Under the body-tested-partition reading, NONE of the\nthree is a floor breach: `lca_pci_conf` tests `writeflag' in its own code but never branches on\n`len' or `func' or `reg', so they induce no partition and fall to clause (e).  **They are closed\nanyway, and the warrant is the CLAIM -> ROW check rather than the floor:** each one falsifies a claim\nthe round's own comment makes -- *\"a type-1 cycle whose bus field is zero selects NOTHING\"* -- and a\nsentence asserting an observable consequence owes a row.\n\n**THE REGISTER PAIR IS ONE SESSION ON PURPOSE.**  A bare *\"reg 4 returns 0\"* row would be vacuous:\nUNMAPPED space also reads 0, which is precisely why the pre-table block deferred it.  Pairing it with\nthe reg-0 read in the SAME process makes the PAIR the oracle -- all-ones then zero, same window, only\nthe register changing -- so a dead window cannot produce it.\n\n*** A SEAT NOTE THAT MATTERS BEYOND THIS ROUND: codex REFUSED THE ASK-AGAIN IN ITS NORMAL FORM, AND\nTHE REFUSAL WAS PREDICTED. ***  The ask-again's whole purpose is to obtain *\"the exact source edit as\na diff hunk\"* that re-opens a closed defect -- construction in its purest form, which\n[[codexcyberflag]] says is filtered.  It was, at 6,339 bytes.  **Reframed to ask for the GAP instead\nof the EDIT** -- *\"name the input-space cell these rows leave least constrained; describe it as a\nproperty, not an edit\"* -- **the same seat answered, and produced the best of the three findings.**\nThe ask-again is structurally a construction task, so every future one needs that reframe.\n\n*** THE LAST TWO SEATS LANDED AND BOTH HIT THE SAME AXIS.  FOUR OF SIX SEATS CONVERGED ON `reg'\nINDEPENDENTLY -- and one of the two is a SHAPE MY OWN MUTANT DOES NOT COVER. ***\n\nThey were still streaming when the panel was first read (grok 230 b, minimax absent) and were NOT\nscored as failures; they finished at 3,600 and 4,549 bytes.  *Sampling once is not a death\ncertificate, and this is the second time today that rule has paid.*\n\n    minimax   `odata = reg == 0? (uint64_t) -1 : 0;`  ->  `odata = (uint64_t) -1;`\n              -- IDENTICAL to `M12_regnull`, which was already in table 3.  Independent arrival at\n              the same mutant is worth recording as corroboration, not as a new finding.\n    grok      `if (type1 && bus == 0)`  ->  `if (type1 && bus == 0 && reg == 0)`\n              *** A DIFFERENT AND STRICTLY WORSE SHAPE. ***\n\n**WHY grok's IS WORSE, and why `M12_regnull` does not cover it.**  `M12` nulls the ternary's\nelse-VALUE: a non-zero register still takes the refusal arm and merely gets the wrong answer.\n**grok's hoists `reg == 0` into the GUARD, so a non-zero register does not take the refusal arm at\nall -- it is FORWARDED, and at 0:7:0 it reaches the real `i82378zb`.**  That re-opens the exact\nmisrouting this round exists to prevent, at every register except 0.\n\n**AND IT COMES WITH THE MAINTAINER'S REASONING, which is what makes it a realistic edit rather than\nan adversarial one** -- grok's own words: *\"A maintainer looking at the ternary and at\n`bus_pci_data_access`'s `dev == NULL` arm will hoist `reg == 0` into the guard: 'the special case is\nonly the ID register; other registers already return 0 for a missing device.'  On an empty slot that\nis even true for reads.  On `i82378zb` it is not.\"*\n\n> **THAT IS THE dev-0 BLINDNESS AGAIN, ONE LEVEL UP.**  The maintainer's premise is TRUE at every\n> empty slot and FALSE at the one populated one -- which is precisely why every row this round added\n> late is aimed at 0:7:0.\n\n**PREDICTED to be caught by `t1 reg4`**: under grok's mutant that access is forwarded to 0:7:0\nregister 4, where the `i82378zb` holds `0x00000003`, against a want of `0x0`.  *** PREDICTED, NOT\nMEASURED -- and this project's rule is that a prediction in a measured column is the frozen-number\nclass.  A targeted two-build table (`base` + `M13_regconj`) is OWED before the round may claim the\nrow kills it. ***\n\n**SEAT TALLY ON THE `reg` AXIS: codex NAMED the gap as the least-constrained cell and ranked it\nabove `len` and `func`; grok and minimax each independently BUILT a mutant on it; and this round's\nown `PRETABLE.out` had DISCLOSED it as clause-(e) debt hours earlier and walked past it.**  Four\nindependent arrivals at one hole is not a coincidence -- it is what an axis every row holds constant\nlooks like from outside.\n\n*** grok PRESCRIBED THE ROW I HAD ALREADY BUILT, AND NAMED TWO RESIDUALS BEYOND IT.  Both are\nDISCLOSED under clause (e) rather than built, and the reasoning for stopping is grok's own. ***\n\nIts closing prescription: *\"If M10_regconj comes back 14/14, the row it wants is a type-1 access at\n0:7:0 **register 4**, both directions, with the same anti-wants as `t1 dev7rd` / `t1 dev7wr` (real\nconfig data / `device 7, function 0 (i82378zb)`).  **A value-0 row at an empty slot will not do.**\"*\n\n**`t1 reg4` IS at 0:7:0 register 4** -- `T1_D7R4 = LCA_PCI_CONF + 0x070080 + BYTE_ENABLES`, re-derived\nas tag 0x3800 -> bus 0, dev 7, func 0, reg 0x04.  So the address half of the prescription was already\nmet, and grok's warning about an empty slot is exactly why the pre-table block had deferred a bare\n`reg != 0` row in the first place.\n\n**RESIDUAL 1 -- ONE DIRECTION, NOT BOTH.**  I built the READ at reg 4 and not the WRITE.  The read\nalone kills `M13_regconj` (the forward returns the device's `0x00000003` against a want of `0x0`), so\nthe mutant in hand is covered; what is NOT covered is a variant that spares reads at non-zero\nregisters and forwards only writes.  **DISCLOSED, not built.**\n\n**RESIDUAL 2 -- THE BUS AXIS, and grok named it while explicitly declining to propose it:**\n\n> *\"Not proposing: `bus = 1` in the type-1 branch (collapses every non-zero bus onto the one\n> `t1 anchor` samples).  That also sweeps, and it is the original cycle-type/bus conflation without\n> the `exit(1)`.  It does not hit 0:7:0 -- those tags get bus 1, which is empty -- so it is a decode\n> collapse, not a reopening of the misrouting.  **A later bus-2 tuple row would catch it; this one\n> does not need that row to be a defect.**\"*\n\n**That is a FOURTH frozen axis** -- every type-1 row uses bus 0 or bus 1, so hardcoding `bus = 1`\nsweeps.  It is a real defect and a LESSER one: it collapses the decode without reaching the populated\nslot.  **A single bus-2 tuple row closes it.**\n\n*** WHY BOTH ARE FILED RATHER THAN BUILT, stated so it is a decision and not an omission. ***  The\ndetector has already grown 4 -> 18 rows inside one round, three of those additions from this single\nask.  Clause (e) exists precisely for this: **a contract that names what it does not cover is\nfalsifiable; one that claims completeness is not.**  grok's own framing supports stopping -- *\"this\none does not need that row to be a defect\"* -- and the probe header will carry both residuals by\nname, so a later escape is *\"outside the contract, as stated\"* rather than an argument about\noverclaiming.\n\n**The round therefore ships an 18-row detector with TWO NAMED HOLES: the write direction at a\nnon-zero register, and any bus value other than 0 or 1.**\n\n*** I PARKED A MAIN-LOOP NOTE IN `entries` AND THE DASHBOARD GENERATOR REFUSED TO RENDER.  It was\nright, and [[sgirandomcolour]] records this exact failure -- which I then repeated. ***\n\n`gen_dashboard.py` stopped with *\"'main-loop' 1 entries across 1 rows: lcaconfbus1kill.  Fix the\nledger (or add the column).  Declared: agy, codex, deepseek, fable5, glm, grok, kimi, minimax,\nopus5\"*.\n\n**`entries` is the SEAT ANSWER list, not a general log.**  `check_stage_panels.py` counts entries to\ndecide whether a stage's nine seats were fired -- so a main-loop note parked there is not merely\nmis-filed, it **MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER HAPPENED**, which is the\nseat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  The entry has been removed and its content lives\nin this note, where it belongs.\n\n*The lesson is not \"remember the convention\".  It is that the generator REFUSES rather than warns,\nso the mistake cannot ship -- and `sgirandomcolour` records seven such entries migrated for the same\nreason on an earlier day.  A rule written in a row's prose did not stop me; the tool did.*",
regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\pass1_20260910\codex.txt:4805:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) and verified:\n\n    for (dev = 0; dev < 21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\n0x800 is a plain int (2^11).  At dev == 20 the expression is 2^31, which overflows a signed 32-bit int --\n**undefined behaviour** in C99, not merely implementation-defined.\n\n**The irony worth recording: this loop is the file's own PRECEDENT for graceful degradation.**  Its\n`dev >= 21' fallback has a commented-out fatal()/exit(1) and executes `dev = 0;' instead -- the\ndelete-the-check-and-substitute-a-safe-default disposition that [[lcabadval]] cites as a transferable\nin-tree warrant.  **The safe path contains UB.**\n\nLOW: `0x800U << dev' fixes it, and on every compiler this project targets the emitted code is already what\nwas intended.  Recorded because a UB shift is exactly what a sanitiser build exists to catch, and\nbuild-asan/ is kept clean -- **so the first question is whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not,\nwhy not.**\n\n*** THE FILED QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER COMES WITH A FREE VEHICLE: THE PROBE SHIPPED FOR\n[[lcaconfbus1kill]] ALREADY EVALUATES THIS EXPRESSION ON EVERY RUN. ***\n\nThis row asks *\"whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not, why not.\"*  Both halves settled\nby reading, 2026-09-07.\n\n**UBSAN IS PRESENT.**  `build-asan/Makefile:9` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`.\n\n**BUT THE SWEEP CANNOT REACH THIS SITE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` runs, per machine type and subtype:\n\n    timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 \"$bin\" -V -E \"$t\" -e \"$s\" \"$DUMMY\" </dev/null\n\nThat is a **CONSTRUCTION** sweep -- it builds each machine and lets it start.  The shift lives in\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s device-scan loop, which runs only when a guest performs a PCI\nCONFIG-SPACE ACCESS.  **Constructing `alphabook1` never executes that loop**, so no amount of\nsweeping flags it.  *This is the witness-ladder distinction one level down: rung 2 (construction)\ncannot reach an access-path defect, which is exactly what the ladder says about registration\nproving presence and not reachability.*\n\n*** AND THE VEHICLE ALREADY EXISTS, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH ACTING ON. ***\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 as the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) drives a\nguest `ldq` into the PCI config window with `ioc_conf == 0`.  That takes the `bus == 0` branch:\n\n    for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\nIts control read uses `relative_addr == 0`, so `tag = (relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff` is **0**, the\n`if` is never true, and **the loop runs all 21 iterations** -- evaluating `0x800 << 20` at the last\none.  `0x800` is `2^11` and a plain `int`, so `0x800 << 20` is `2^31`: **signed overflow, C99\n6.5.7p4, undefined behaviour, on every single run of that probe.**\n\n> **SO THE MEASUREMENT IS: run the existing `lca_confbus_probe.py` against `build-asan/gxemul` and\n> read stderr for a UBSAN shift diagnostic.  No new probe, no new row, no new apparatus.**  The\n> expected diagnostic names `dev_lca.c` and a left-shift of a signed value.\n\n**NOT RUN YET** -- a probe run must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.  Queued.\n\n**IF IT FIRES**, this row gains a measured witness and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) can be\ngraded properly.  **IF IT DOES NOT**, that is itself a finding about\n[[ubsanshiftlane]] -- it would mean the ASAN build is not reporting a shift UB that is provably\nevaluated, and the whole proposed UBSAN differential lane rests on an assumption that does not hold.\n**Either outcome is worth the one run**, which is the argument for doing it before designing the lane.\n\n*Found while answering this row's own filed question rather than by looking for it -- and note the\nirony the row already recorded: this loop is the file's OWN graceful-degradation precedent, cited by\n[[lcabadval]] as a transferable warrant.  The safe path contains the UB.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE UBSAN DIAGNOSTIC FIRES, NAMING THE FILE, LINE, COLUMN, VALUE AND\nSHIFT COUNT.  Artifact: `regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n    GXemul> step 5\n    fffffc0001040000:  243f0102    ldah  t0,0x102\n    fffffc0001040004:  a4410010    ldq   t1,16(t0)\n    fffffc0001040008:  a4810000    ldq   t3,0(t0)\n    fffffc000104000c:  a4210008    ldq   t0,8(t0)\n    fffffc0001040010:  a4620000    ldq   t2,0(t1)\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n    GXemul> print t2\n    0xffffffffffffffff\n\n**`2048` is `0x800`; `by 20 places` is the final loop iteration; `dev_lca.c:125` is exactly\n`if (tag & (0x800 << dev))`.**  The diagnostic lands on the guest `ldq t2,0(t1)` -- a real guest\ninstruction through real address decode on an unmodified `alphabook1`, with the empty-slot signature\n`0xffffffffffffffff` printed immediately after.\n\n**NO NEW APPARATUS WAS BUILT.**  This is `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, shipped earlier the same day\nas the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]], run against `build-asan/gxemul` instead of `build/gxemul`.\nThe control read takes the `bus == 0` branch with `tag == 0`, so the scan loop runs all 21\niterations and reaches the overflow every time.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST MEASUREMENT WAS A FALSE NEGATIVE OF MY OWN MAKING, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE\nRESULT. ***  Run 1 reported NO diagnostic and I nearly recorded that as a finding against\n[[ubsanshiftlane]].  The cause: **I grepped the PROBE's stdout, and a UBSAN message goes to the\nEMULATOR's stderr**, which lands in the probe's internal pty buffer and is never echoed.  That is\n*exactly* the defect I had fixed hours earlier for the `bus attrib` row -- a green row whose evidence\nnever reaches the artifact -- recurring one level out, on the harness rather than the row.\n\n> **A PROBE ONLY SHOWS YOU WHAT IT CHOOSES TO PRINT.  When you are looking for output the probe was\n> not written to surface, dump the buffer -- do not grep the summary.**  Re-run from a frozen COPY\n> in `/tmp` (the shipped probe is not edited for a measurement) with the full buffer echoed, and the\n> diagnostic was in the first ten lines.\n\n*The wrong answer REASSURED, which is this project's recurring signature: \"no diagnostic\" is exactly\nwhat a clean build looks like.*\n\n**THIS ROW NOW HAS A MEASURED WITNESS** and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) is gradeable.\nThe repair is COMPLETE for this site: `0x800U` makes the operand unsigned, so `2048U << 20` is\n`0x80000000U`, well inside `unsigned int`, and the `tag &` comparison is unaffected because `tag` is\nalready a wider unsigned quantity.\n\n**SEVERITY STAYS LOW and that is deliberate**: the overflow is UB by the language, the emitted code\non every compiler this project targets is what was intended, and no wrong guest-visible value has\nbeen measured.  What changed is that it is now WITNESSED rather than argued.\n\n*** THE FIX IS DESIGNED AND EXECUTABLE.  Written out here so the next session can run it rather than\nre-derive it -- the round is small and every input is now in hand. ***\n\n**THE EDIT, one token:**\n\n    -\tif (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n    +\tif (tag & (0x800U << dev))\t/*  #NNN  */\n\n`0x800U` makes the operand `unsigned int`, so `2048U << 20` is `0x80000000U` -- well inside\n`unsigned int` -- and no other behaviour moves: `tag` is already a wider unsigned quantity, so the\n`&` and the truth value are unchanged at every one of the 21 iterations.  **The emitted code on\nevery compiler this project targets is what was already intended**, which is why the severity is LOW\neven though the language calls it undefined.\n\n*** THE TEST-FIRST ORDER IS ALREADY SATISFIED, WHICH IS UNUSUAL AND WORTH NOTING. ***  The witness\nexists and is RED on the committed build: running `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` against\n`build-asan/gxemul` emits\n\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n\nSo the reproduce-before-you-edit step is DONE and preserved\n(`regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`).  The post-fix half is: the same command,\nsame binary path, and **the diagnostic must be ABSENT**.  Same script, opposite outcome -- which is\nthe ladder's clause (i) exactly.\n\n**WHAT THE ROUND OWES, and the one real decision in it:**\n\nThe two-direction control above is a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL, and this project has that filed as its own\nround ([[ubsanshiftlane]]) because it is a NEW MEASUREMENT LANE -- a second binary and a STDERR\noracle rather than a value oracle.  **So the honest choice is:**\n\n* **(a) ship the fix with the UBSAN differential as its detector**, which means building at least a\n  minimal version of the lane in this round -- and a lane added inside a fix round is a lane nobody\n  reviewed, which is the exact objection `ubsanshiftlane` was filed to avoid; or\n* **(b) ship the fix with a SOURCE PIN** -- a check asserting the expression is `0x800U` -- and\n  leave the differential to its own round.  `#510`'s own product comments already name a source pin\n  as the legitimate alternative to UBSAN for precisely this class (\"No row can kill a `1U' -> `1'\n  regression; UBSAN or a source pin can\").\n\n**(b) is in scope and (a) is not**, on this project's own stopping rule: the fix is a one-token\ncorrectness repair, and expanding it into a new measurement lane is the scope creep the rule exists\nto refuse.  *Recorded as a reasoned choice rather than a preference, so a reviewer can disagree with\nthe reason.*\n\n**COST: a build.**  The edit must propagate to all four trees and `build-asan` must be REBUILT for\nthe post-fix measurement, which is the only expensive part and is why this was not executed the\nmoment it was designed -- a rebuild must not overlap a running seat.\n\n**AND THE POST-FIX RUN IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIX.**  If the diagnostic does NOT\ndisappear, the repair is wrong or the site is not the one being reported, and that is a finding\nabout the measurement rather than a formality.  Predict-then-measure: it should vanish, and nothing\nelse in the probe's four rows should move (3/4 before, 3/4 after -- the `bus kill` row is a\ndifferent defect and stays RED).\n\n*** FIXED AND MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, 2026-09-07, as `#511`. ***\n\n    HEAD    1 diagnostic     ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw\n    FIXED   0 diagnostics    ubsan_FIXED_20260907.raw\n    control  build/ scores 3/4 before AND after, same three rows green, `bus kill' still RED\n             (a different defect) -- NOTHING ELSE MOVED\n\n**The pre-registered prediction held exactly**: the diagnostic vanished and no other row moved.  All\nfour trees cleared two-part after the propagate (`est/`: source tree, part 2 N/A).\n\n**The detector is the differential itself and it is NOT GATE-WIRED**, which the CHANGELOG block says\nin those words.  A source pin was considered and rejected: it would test the TEXT while this round\nhas the BEHAVIOUR.  Gate-wiring is [[ubsanshiftlane]], deliberately separate.\n\n*** THE SIBLING QUESTION IS ANSWERED BEFORE THE PANEL ANSWERS IT, AND THE RESULT IS A CLEAN\nABSENCE -- RECORDED WITH ITS WARRANT SO IT IS FALSIFIABLE. ***\n\n`#511`'s own pass-2 brief asks (Q5) whether a sibling of this shape was missed.  Censused:\n\n    signed-constant  <<  VARIABLE, tree-wide                       317 sites\n    of those, literal > 1 AND headroom <= 24                          2 sites\n\n**317 IS A CORRELATE, NOT THE CLASS.**  `1 << x` with 31 places of headroom is the ordinary bitmask\nidiom in C and is a defect only if `x` can reach 31.  The `#511` SHAPE is distinctive: a literal\nLARGER than 1 (so little headroom) shifted by a variable that a loop can drive to the overflow point.\nFiltering on that leaves TWO:\n\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:772   tmp &= 0x0fff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 20\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:774   tmp &= 0xffff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 16\n\n**BOTH ARE SAFE, and the warrant is a compile-time constant:**\n\n    src/include/cop0.h:85    #define PAGEMASK_SHIFT   13\n    0x0fff << 13 = 0x01ffe000       0xffff << 13 = 0x1fffe000       INT_MAX = 0x7fffffff\n\n`PAGEMASK_SHIFT` is a MACRO, not a runtime variable, so the compiler folds the whole expression and\nthere is no shift to overflow.  **My regex matched an identifier and could not tell a macro from a\nvariable** -- which is why the two hits needed reading rather than counting.\n\n> **[THIS ABSENCE RECORD IS FALSE.  See the correction at the end of this note -- the grok pass-2\n> seat found TWO siblings my filter had hidden.]**  ~~no other site in the tree has a signed literal > 1 shifted by a value that can reach 2^31.~~  THE WARRANT IS\n> `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` AND ITS BEING A COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT.**  If either changes -- the value\n> raised past 15, or the macro replaced by a variable -- the two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites become\n> live and this record expires.\n\n*A near-miss worth keeping: my first attempt grepped `define PAGEMASK_SHIFT` in `src/` and returned\nNOTHING, and I had already computed the arithmetic assuming 13.  Zero hits is not evidence, and the\nnumber I was about to rely on was a guess.  It IS 13 -- found in `src/include/cop0.h` on a broader\nsearch -- but the conclusion was reached before the fact was.*\n\n*** THE ABSENCE RECORD ABOVE IS FALSE, AND MY OWN FILTER IS WHAT MADE IT SO.  The grok pass-2 seat\nfound TWO SIBLINGS, both verified here. ***\n\ngrok's verdict on `#511` closes with: *\"Two other PCI IDSEL scanners still evaluate `1 << 31`.\"*\nVerified by reading:\n\n    dev_uninorth.c:70   for (dev=11; dev<32; dev++)\n                 :71       if (idata & (1 << dev))            <- dev REACHES 31\n    dev_v3.c:164        for (dev=24; dev<32; dev++)\n            :165           if (relative_addr & (1 << dev))    <- dev REACHES 31\n\n    1 << 31 = 0x80000000 = 2147483648    INT_MAX = 2147483647    OVERFLOWS\n\n**Both are the IDENTICAL SHAPE to `#511`** -- a signed literal shifted by a loop variable the loop\nbound drives to the overflow point -- and both loops explicitly run to `dev < 32`, so 31 is reached\nwhenever no earlier bit matches.\n\n*** THE FILTER THAT HID THEM WAS MINE, AND IT WAS EXACTLY BACKWARDS. ***  My census excluded\n`if v <= 1: continue`, reasoning that ``1 << x`` is the ordinary bitmask idiom in C and only a\ndefect if `x` reaches 31.  **That reasoning is right and the exclusion is wrong**: `1` has the MOST\nheadroom (31 places), which makes it the ONLY literal that overflows at the maximum shift a\n`< 32` loop can produce.  I filtered on \"little headroom = suspicious\" and thereby removed the\nsingle literal whose headroom exactly matches the commonest loop bound in the tree.\n\n> **AN ABSENCE RECORD IS ONLY AS GOOD AS THE FILTER THAT PRODUCED IT, AND MY WARRANT NAMED THE\n> WRONG THING.**  I recorded the warrant as `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` -- a fact about the two sites the\n> filter DID surface -- when the load-bearing assumption was the FILTER ITSELF.  A warrant should\n> name what would falsify the record, and what falsified it was the exclusion, not the constant.\n\n**The `PAGEMASK_SHIFT` half stands**: those two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites really are safe, for the\nreason given.  What is retracted is the universal claim that nothing else in the tree has the shape.\n\n*Filed to [[shiftubcensus]], which is the row for this class and was already marked HONESTLY\nPARTIAL.  Recorded here too because THIS row is where the false absence was written.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*",
