1560:# `#510` armbfxwidth32: UBFX and SBFX built their mask with an undefined shift
1561:
1562:**THE DEFECT.** `cpu_arm_instr.c`. The decoder gives both handlers
1563:`width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)` (`:4486`, `:4493`), so **`width` reaches 32** �?" and `1` is a
1564:plain `int`:
1565:
1566:    :1316  ubfx   uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
1567:    :1340  sbfx   uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
1568:    :1344  sbfx   uint32_t topBitMask = 1 << (width-1);
1569:
1570:`1 << 32` shifts by the full width of the type (C99 6.5.7p3); `1 << 31` overflows a signed `int`
1571:(6.5.7p4). Both are undefined. `UBFX rd, rn, #0, #32` is a legal A32 encoding �?" the architectural
1572:constraint is `lsb + width <= 32`, which `lsb = 0, width = 32` satisfies �?" so this is
1573:guest-reachable, not a decoder curiosity.
1574:
1575:> ### *** ONLY THE WIDTH-32 CASE IS OBSERVABLE, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED. ***
1576:>
1577:> Three pass-1 seats said the defect is "two widths wide". That is right about the LANGUAGE and
1578:> needs a qualifier about the SYMPTOM. With `r1 = 0x89abcdef`:
1579:>
1580:>     width  correct       on the committed build   observable?
1581:>     32     0x89abcdef    0x00000000               YES -- the whole register becomes zero
1582:>     31     0x09abcdef    0x09abcdef               no  -- same answer
1583:>
1584:> **At 31 the expression is undefined TWICE and wraps back to the right answer:** `1 << 31` gives
1585:> `INT_MIN`, and `INT_MIN - 1` gives `INT_MAX` = `0x7fffffff`, which IS the correct 31-bit mask.
1586:> **The repair is owed by the language at both widths; only one of them was returning a wrong
1587:> value**, and the round's severity sentence says so rather than claiming both.
1588:
1589:**THE FIX**, at all three sites:
1590:
1591:    uint32_t mask = (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);
1592:
1593:    if (width < 32 && (x & (1U << (width - 1))))       /*  sbfx, hoisted above the shift  */
1594:            x |= ~mask;
1595:
1596:**`1U` is load-bearing and not style: it is what defends width 31; the guard defends 32.** That
1597:distinction was itself a correction �?" the round's own brief claimed the m88k idiom is safe because
1598:of its GUARD, and three seats pointed out the CAST is what covers `w == 31` there.
1599:
1600:At `sbfx`, the `width < 32` test that would have made `topBitMask` moot **sat on the line after
1601:the shift that needed it**. Reordering the `&&` is semantically identical for every width in
1602:`{1..32}` �?" both operands are ordinary locals with no side effects �?" and short-circuits before
1603:the shift.
1604:
