1560:# `#510` armbfxwidth32: UBFX and SBFX built their mask with an undefined shift
1561:
1562:**THE DEFECT.** `cpu_arm_instr.c`. The decoder gives both handlers
1563:`width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)` (`:4486`, `:4493`), so **`width` reaches 32** �?" and `1` is a
1564:plain `int`:
1565:
1566:    :1316  ubfx   uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
1567:    :1340  sbfx   uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
1568:    :1344  sbfx   uint32_t topBitMask = 1 << (width-1);
1569:
1570:`1 << 32` shifts by the full width of the type (C99 6.5.7p3); `1 << 31` overflows a signed `int`
1571:(6.5.7p4). Both are undefined. `UBFX rd, rn, #0, #32` is a legal A32 encoding �?" the architectural
1572:constraint is `lsb + width <= 32`, which `lsb = 0, width = 32` satisfies �?" so this is
1573:guest-reachable, not a decoder curiosity.
1574:
1575:> ### *** ONLY THE WIDTH-32 CASE IS OBSERVABLE, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED. ***
1576:>
1577:> Three pass-1 seats said the defect is "two widths wide". That is right about the LANGUAGE and
1578:> needs a qualifier about the SYMPTOM. With `r1 = 0x89abcdef`:
1579:>
1580:>     width  correct       on the committed build   observable?
1581:>     32     0x89abcdef    0x00000000               YES -- the whole register becomes zero
1582:>     31     0x09abcdef    0x09abcdef               no  -- same answer
1583:>
1584:> **At 31 the expression is undefined TWICE and wraps back to the right answer:** `1 << 31` gives
1585:> `INT_MIN`, and `INT_MIN - 1` gives `INT_MAX` = `0x7fffffff`, which IS the correct 31-bit mask.
1586:> **The repair is owed by the language at both widths; only one of them was returning a wrong
1587:> value**, and the round's severity sentence says so rather than claiming both.
1588:
1589:**THE FIX**, at all three sites:
1590:
1591:    uint32_t mask = (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);
1592:
1593:    if (width < 32 && (x & (1U << (width - 1))))       /*  sbfx, hoisted above the shift  */
1594:            x |= ~mask;
1595:
1596:**`1U` is load-bearing and not style: it is what defends width 31; the guard defends 32.** That
1597:distinction was itself a correction �?" the round's own brief claimed the m88k idiom is safe because
1598:of its GUARD, and three seats pointed out the CAST is what covers `w == 31` there.
1599:
1600:At `sbfx`, the `width < 32` test that would have made `topBitMask` moot **sat on the line after
1601:the shift that needed it**. Reordering the `&&` is semantically identical for every width in
1602:`{1..32}` �?" both operands are ordinary locals with no side effects �?" and short-circuits before
1603:the shift.
1604:
1605:**THE FORK'S OWN IDIOM**, found by running the grep-the-fork step before writing the questions
1606:rather than after: `cpu_m88k_instr.c:532` and `:539`, in `X(clr)` and `X(set)` �?"
1607:`w == 0 ? 0xffffffff : ((uint32_t)1 << w) - 1`, where the m88k encoding spells full width as `0`.
1608:
1609:**WITNESS, rung 3, unmodified `-E testarm`:** `regress/arm_bfx_width_probe.py`. A cold guest
1610:plants one instruction word with `put w`, sets `pc`, steps once, and reads `r0` and `pc` back.
1611:
1612:    HEAD  9/11   witness_HEAD_20260907.raw       FIX  11/11  witness_FIXED_20260907.raw
1613:                                                    FIX  14/14  witness_FIXED14_20260907.raw
1614:
1615:    *** A CITATION OF MINE DECAYED INSIDE ONE EVENING -- AND GIT GAVE IT BACK, WHICH IS THE
1616:    PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  The follow-up re-ran the probe and MY DRIVER REUSED THE FILENAME
1617:    `witness_FIXED12_20260907.raw' with a `>' redirect, so the working-tree copy stopped holding
1618:    the 12-row run and held a 14-row one instead.  For about an hour this block cited a file that
1619:    said something ELSE -- not unverifiable, but CONTRADICTED BY ITS OWN EVIDENCE, which is the
1620:    more detectable form and is how it was caught.
1621:
1622:    **The artifact was COMMITTED, so `git checkout' restored it byte-for-byte and the 12/12 score
1623:    is genuine and citable.**  All four numbers below stand on their own logs: 9/11, 11/11, 12/12
1624:    and 14/14.  I first wrote this paragraph saying the measurement was GONE; that was wrong, and
1625:    a correction carries more authority than the error it corrects, so it earns a second reading
1626:    rather than less of one.
1627:
1628:    **The rule this vindicates is not the one I reached for.**  "A measurement cited in a durable
1710:## Pass 2 ratified the repair and refused the comments
1711:
1712:`panel_20260907_192452`, six seats. **grok's verdict: RATIFY THE REPAIR; DO NOT RATIFY THE
1713:COMMENTS, THE "11/11 PINS BOTH HALVES" READING, OR THE 496-COUNT.** All four packet-fed seats
1714:independently confirmed the mask is correct at 1, 31 and 32 and that the `&&` reorder changes no
1715:guest value.
1716:
1717:*** THREE ONE-LINE ESCAPES, AND THE VALUABLE PART IS WHY TWO OF THEM CANNOT BE CLOSED. ***
1718:
1719:| escape | why it survived a full score |
1720:|---|---|
1721:| `1U` -> `1` in the ternary | `w31` stays green �?" **it is the very wrap this round's own comment describes** �?" and `w32` never evaluates the shift. `1 << 31` and `1U << 31` give the SAME mask after the `- 1`. |
1722:| restore `topBitMask` above the `if` | `s w32` stays green: with a correct 32-bit mask `x \|= ~mask` is `x \|= 0`. **`s w32` pins the MASK, not `topBitMask` and not the hoist** �?" the probe comment calling it "the second-UB row" was wrong about what that row can see. |
1723:| `if (width < 32)` with no bit test | `s mid` stays green because **every byte field of `0x89abcdef` has bit 7 set** (`0x89 0xab 0xcd 0xef`). No row had an SBFX field with the top bit CLEAR. |
1724:
1725:**The third is closed in-round** by `s clear` �?" `SBFX #1,#4` gives field `0x7`, bit 3 clear, so a
1726:correct build returns `0x7` and an always-extend regression returns `0xfffffff7`.
1727:
1728:**The first two are DISCLOSED, because they are unkillable by a guest-value row on principle:**
1729:both are DEFINEDNESS repairs, and definedness has no guest-visible value on a non-trapping host.
1730:*** THEY ARE NOT UNKILLABLE IN PRINCIPLE: `build-asan` carries `-fsanitize=address,undefined`, so
1731:a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL pins both �?" fixed source produces no shift diagnostic, the `1U`->`1` mutant
1732:produces one. That is a second binary and a STDERR oracle, i.e. a new measurement lane, so it is
1733:filed as `ubsanshiftlane` for its own round rather than bolted onto one whose pass 2 has run. ***
1734:
1735:**AND ONE CLAIMED BREACH WAS REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT.** glm asserted *"no row ever takes the
1736:sign-extension branch, so deleting `x |= ~mask` still scores 11/11."* The mutant was built with a
1737:pre-registered prediction and measured **10/11, killed by `s mid` alone** �?" `s mid`'s field is
1738:`0xde`, whose bit 7 IS set, so the branch is taken. Restore verified, sentinel cleared.
1739:
1740:### Five comment overclaims, corrected in place
1741:
1742:1. *"the mask became 0"* and *"`1 << 31` gives INT_MIN"* �?" these are **this host's lowering**
1743:   (`shl` takes its count mod 32), not C99 values. Undefined behaviour has no defined result to
1744:   measure. The rows observe the RESULT REGISTER, never the mask.
1745:2. The `sbfx` block was a **verbatim copy of the ubfx one** and quoted a UBFX measurement �?" a
1746:   sentence about the other function.
1747:3. *"the `&&` reorder is semantically identical"* **understates it.** The old form computed
1748:   `1 << (width-1)` as a PRIOR STATEMENT, and a guard never rescues an expression already
1749:   evaluated. The guest value is identical; DEFINEDNESS at 32 is not. **And with `1U` the shift is
1750:   already defined at 32, so the hoist is load-bearing for a signed `1` and redundant for `1U` �?"
1751:   calling them equivalent defences was wrong.**
1752:4. *"there is no sign to extend"* at width 32 is the wrong reason �?" a 32-bit field HAS a sign bit;
1753:   the body is a no-op because `~mask == 0`.
1754:5. **The 496 count** �?" see below.
1755:
1756:### The count was wrong, and two seats caught it independently
1757:
1758:This round claimed *"496 of 1024 encodings give a NEGATIVE shift count"* for `bfi`. Enumerated:
1759:
1760:    width < 0   465      width == 0   31      width <= 0   496      msb < lsb   496
1761:
1762:**496 is the `msb < lsb` population.** 465 are strictly negative; the other 31 give width exactly
1763:0, where `1 << 0` is DEFINED and the mask becomes 0 �?" semantically wrong, not undefined. glm
1764:derived it from the brief's own width formula and grok reached it separately. *The conclusion is
1765:unchanged; the number was wrong, and it had already propagated into four records.*
1766:
1767:### And my own patch invalidated my own citations
1768:
1769:grok: the brief's decoder cites `:4486`/`:4493` were the PRE-comment numbers; after the diff they
1770:were `:4525`/`:4532`, off by exactly the lines this comment-heavy patch inserts. After the pass-2
1771:edits they moved again, to `:4540`/`:4547`. **Three values in one evening for one unchanged
1772:expression** �?" which is why the carrier's rule is to cite by CONSTRUCT, not by line, and why no
1773:line number for that expression appears in this block.
1774:
18101:### `mipsextshift` (MED) -- filed 2026-09-10
18102:
18103:`cpu_mips_instr.c`, anchors `X(ext)` and `X(dext)`: the extraction shift count is computed from two
18104:independently decoded instruction fields and **goes NEGATIVE for 496 of 1024 encodable pairs**.
18105:
18106:`X(ext)` computes `int size = msbd + 1;` then `x = (rs << (32-lsb-size)) >> (32-lsb-size);`, with `msbd`
18107:and `lsb` each decoded from the instruction word in `0..31`. Enumerating all 1024 pairs gives **496 with a
18108:negative count** -- undefined, C99 6.5.7p3. `rs` is `uint32_t`, so the left operand is unsigned and the
18109:signed-`1` form does not arise; **that is the trap**, because a reader checking *"is this unsigned?"* passes
18110:the site, and a negative COUNT is undefined whatever the operand's type.
18111:
18112:`X(dext)` is the same shape at 64 bits and is worse in a way that reads as a precaution: the count is CAST,
18113:`(rs << (uint64_t)(64-lsb-size))`. **The cast does not rescue it** -- a negative `int` converted to
18114:`uint64_t` becomes an enormous positive value, so the count is then at or beyond the width of the type.
18115:*A cast that converts one undefined form into another while reading as a fix is worth recording as a
18116:pattern.*
18117:
18118:**This is a FIELD-VALIDATION defect, not a cast defect, and that decides the fix.** MIPS requires
18119:`lsb + size <= 32` for EXT and the corresponding bound for the `dext` family; an encoding violating it is
18120:architecturally invalid. So the repair is probably to DECLINE or to raise the architectural exception, not
18121:to paper the shift over -- a mask repair would give a defined answer to a question the guest was not
18122:entitled to ask, and would make invalid encodings look supported. That is a panel question, which is why
18123:this is filed MED rather than as an easy one-token repair.
18124:
18125:Reachability: MIPS is this project's primary architecture with the most exercised rigs, and the count comes
18126:straight from the instruction word with no CPU-model gate, so a guest need only execute one malformed EXT.
18127:**Not measured, no witness built** -- what is established is the domain arithmetic and the decode, both
18128:re-derived here.
18129:
18130:**Distinct from `shiftubcensus`**, which is the SIGNED-1 family (`1 << n` with a plain-`int` literal). This
18131:is the NEGATIVE-COUNT family: a different C99 clause and a different fix shape. Found by the GPT-6 Astra
18132:fix-design review on `armbfiwidth` while looking for siblings of that round's idiom outside ARM.
18133:
18134:### `mapledmaaddr` (HIGH) -- filed 2026-09-10
18135:
18136:`dev_dreamcast_maple.c`, grep anchor `case 0x04:	/*  MAPLE_DMAADDR  */`: **the read arm computes the
18137:correct value and then throws it away by terminating the process.** In order:
18138:
18139:```c
18140:fatal("[ dreamcast_maple: TODO: read from dmaaddr ]\n");
18141:odata = d->dmaaddr;
18142:exit(1);
18143:```
18144:
18145:The WRITE arm two lines above stores `d->dmaaddr = idata`, so the register is **fully modelled in both
18146:directions**. A guest that writes the DMA address and reads it back -- the ordinary way to confirm a
18147:controller programming step -- is killed on the read. `machine_dreamcast.c` adds `dreamcast_maple`
18148:unconditionally, and nothing has to start DMA or build a command list to get there.
18149:
18150:**This is option-set member 4 (ACCEPT) in its textbook shape**: the model already computes the right
18151:answer and then discards it, so the disposition is to delete the kill and KEEP THE VALUE, with **no
18152:latch** -- there is no event to announce, and a bounded announce would ship a wrong record in product code

